com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqtkiCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAARGoQACijbWhX+0GyRyJBQkeh4lUM\nGlVr7Q7GLSKE0ZnoLz9bee5TE+fkQrIAnOpsSGQA+/lLEP0wagPxEUuQoYw1s0ZH\nXFhaze6c4CBb6nsKZJ9DzKwwgAY9MaCORqT35qO9Y7lBgnbtaWVczjnZu0g2MwGi\nuPixTYW26tSbeyKobY6aUekc51WTGQsHX6cMprfd0J1jJsCxfTQzibHuN/5qEUKG\nHUec5EE91z/pll2AU7ry4HShLPUizXyOq4v8UORSm5Gc+2mpMq/2qwbIi2WX+bFg\nhsHkB1aOfjGSlFCayMwujNOpdwRlPx0Cbfm6SGHOu8EGg+CWTtmwmc7NAqF3WEPM\nlx3e1DhnVyJegqd+Frlea4uq17UXC4xNXyyiVvdP5DCs0aWxB0L1yMATXkTVlV8a\nsE5jERd3YqZ+e+cCN+j4HfDaGWXeyr8LIZ0OKwu0G7KRHdZvHzdiob1UJqwRv1lj\nGtAtdf0Orq1Ot+mkR+T6O4IWM3DhobnefE4KlLYXanLLQYKlKBwVZEjOyqPlZ8Yy\nfcYXdsXeUoTsKWPpHA9vk+OK6wzm8opDqG/3YcVNR816L9hRPHcyPyDPgqrIKcXg\nmcL6aNBKmeUWQvvIhWYc2QWrnHLoJ3YIhfyMBXqmT/RLxf/QBws7n1UKF8HhZFAG\noW7C6r1W0fCoRtA4Pg96\n=zj6a\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T18:00:03Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/67b2090802c76af97e6e76e584e8d04b28a1435b","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDY3YjIwOTA4MDJjNzZhZjk3ZTZlNzZlNTg0ZThkMDRiMjhhMTQzNWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/4f3106457c518aca7e7e46143d30bcebd1af4031","sha":"4f3106457c518aca7e7e46143d30bcebd1af4031","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/4f3106457c518aca7e7e46143d30bcebd1af4031"}],"sha":"67b2090802c76af97e6e76e584e8d04b28a1435b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/67b2090802c76af97e6e76e584e8d04b28a1435b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/4f3106457c518aca7e7e46143d30bcebd1af4031/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T17:34:16Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T17:34:16Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: gate the two GET endpoints on the site permission (JAHIA-SEC-432) (#27)\n\nCloses the finding tracked as **JAHIA-SEC-432** (YWH report 892184,\nJahia/jahia-security-scan#1662), whose `remediation.fix_status` is\n`none` with an empty `prs[]` — nothing was in flight for it.\n\n## The defect\n\n`GeoReportServlet.doGet` and `CrawlerCheckServlet.doGet` checked whether\nthe caller was the guest user, and nothing else. That is a question\nabout **authentication**. What the endpoints answer with is operator\nconfiguration, so the question had to be about **authorisation**.\n\nConfirmed live on 8.2.4.0-SNAPSHOT with canary values planted in the\nmodule''s OSGi config, read by an account that answers 403 on\n`/jahia/jcontent`:\n\n| endpoint | what an unprivileged account was told |\n|---|---|\n| `GET /modules/geo-readiness/report` | the configured AI provider and\nmodel |\n| `GET /modules/geo-readiness/crawler-check` | the full crawler\nuser-agent list |\n\nThe AI **API key was never returned**, and nothing site-specific was.\nWhat leaks is which third party the operator sends site content to — a\ndata-residency fact some customers treat as confidential — and the exact\nuser agents the site handles specially, which helps anyone shaping a\nrequest to be treated as a crawler.\n\n## It is not the three-line fix it looks like\n\n`SiteScope.require(path, language, anyOf)` resolves the site that **owns\na path**. Neither GET had a path — they took no parameters at all — so\nthere was nothing to hand it.\n\nGating them is therefore a contract change. Both GETs now require `path`\nand `language` on the query string, exactly as the POST handlers already\nrequire them in their JSON body, and the three front-end call sites send\nthem. The returned values stay global to the module: the site is what\nthe caller must hold `publish` **on**, not what is looked up.\n\n## Where the gate lives\n\nIn `GeoServlet`, as `requireDashboard` — not pasted into both servlets.\nThis would have been the **third** copy of resolve-site-or-deny, and\nthat base class exists precisely because four copies of seven helpers\ndrifted apart and hid two real defects between them.\n\n`isGuest()` is removed. It had exactly one caller, which this PR\nchanges, and leaving the wrong-question helper sitting in the shared\nbase is how the same defect comes back. `requireUser` is what the POST\npaths already use. The bundle declares no `Export-Package`, so nothing\noutside it could call `isGuest`.\n\n## Why it survived three releases\n\n> **Correction.** An earlier version of this description said *\"nothing\never tested a GET\"*. That was wrong, and CI caught it. `report.spec.ts`\ncalled `GET /report` three times, directly through `cy.request` rather\nthan the `geoPost` helper — which is why grepping the helper missed\nthem. `crawler-check`''s GET really was never called.\n\nThe truth is worse than no coverage. One of those three tests was:\n\n```ts\nit(''may read the status'', () => {\n    cy.request({url: ENDPOINTS.report, ...}).then(response => {\n        expectJsonOk(response, ''the status only says whether a provider exists'');\n    });\n});\n```\n\nA contributor — `publication-start` but not `publish` — reading the\nstatus, **asserted as correct and passing on every run**. The defect was\nnot uncovered; it was *pinned as intended behaviour*.\n\nIts stated reason is wrong on its own terms: \"the status only says\nwhether a provider exists\" describes a body that also carries `provider`\nand `model`, and the assertion eleven lines above it spells out all\nthree keys. The test was written from what the endpoint did rather than\nfrom what it should do — which is how a characterisation test quietly\nbecomes a specification.\n\nThat test now asserts `403` and that the body carries no provider.\n\n## The new coverage\n\n`geoGet` is added, and `authorization.spec.ts` walks **both** GETs for\nthree actors:\n\n| actor | expected | why it is in the suite |\n|---|---|---|\n| guest | 401 | was already correct — proves the gate is not simply off\n|\n| **contributor** | **403** | **the regression test.** Holds\n`publication-start` but not `publish` |\n| publisher | 200 | without it, every refusal is consistent with a gate\nthat refuses everybody |\n\nThe middle row is the point. These endpoints were never open to\nanonymous callers, so a suite checking only guest-refused and\npublisher-allowed would have stayed green throughout the defect''s life.\nOnly an authenticated-but-unprivileged actor separates authentication\nfrom authorisation — and the fixture already had one. Each refusal also\nasserts the payload field is **absent**, because the discriminator here\nis the response body, not the status.\n\nA fourth case asserts `400 path required` when no site is named, and a\nnew guest case sends **no** query string at all and still expects 401 —\npinning the order, so an anonymous caller cannot tell \"malformed path\"\nfrom \"not yours\" and use the pair to discover which sites exist.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `mvn clean install` — **354 unit tests green**\n- [x] `tsc --noEmit` — no errors in `authorization.spec.ts`,\n`report.spec.ts` or `support/geo.ts`\n- [x] **Cypress e2e run locally in full** via `ci.build.sh` +\n`ci.startup.sh`\n\n## Notes for the reviewer\n\n- **`crawlerCheck.getStatus()` has no importer** anywhere in the product\n— the `crawler-check` GET has no front-end caller at all. It is updated\nfor consistency rather than deleted, since removing it is a separate\ndecision.\n- `tsc --noEmit` reports **9 pre-existing** implicit-`any` errors in\n`scan-outputs.spec.ts` and `visibility.spec.ts`, untouched here. No\nworkflow runs `typecheck`, so they are latent rather than blocking.\nHappy to clear them in their own PR.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"425c5f1e42a8dc7ea3175fcd405e2b5478b1f579","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/425c5f1e42a8dc7ea3175fcd405e2b5478b1f579"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/4f3106457c518aca7e7e46143d30bcebd1af4031","verification":{"payload":"tree 425c5f1e42a8dc7ea3175fcd405e2b5478b1f579\nparent 51eec32a38b29dd69e4c3d0f7c0a6256e5a3accf\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789580056 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789580056 +0200\n\nfix: gate the two GET endpoints on the site permission (JAHIA-SEC-432) (#27)\n\nCloses the finding tracked as **JAHIA-SEC-432** (YWH report 892184,\nJahia/jahia-security-scan#1662), whose `remediation.fix_status` is\n`none` with an empty `prs[]` — nothing was in flight for it.\n\n## The defect\n\n`GeoReportServlet.doGet` and `CrawlerCheckServlet.doGet` checked whether\nthe caller was the guest user, and nothing else. That is a question\nabout **authentication**. What the endpoints answer with is operator\nconfiguration, so the question had to be about **authorisation**.\n\nConfirmed live on 8.2.4.0-SNAPSHOT with canary values planted in the\nmodule''s OSGi config, read by an account that answers 403 on\n`/jahia/jcontent`:\n\n| endpoint | what an unprivileged account was told |\n|---|---|\n| `GET /modules/geo-readiness/report` | the configured AI provider and\nmodel |\n| `GET /modules/geo-readiness/crawler-check` | the full crawler\nuser-agent list |\n\nThe AI **API key was never returned**, and nothing site-specific was.\nWhat leaks is which third party the operator sends site content to — a\ndata-residency fact some customers treat as confidential — and the exact\nuser agents the site handles specially, which helps anyone shaping a\nrequest to be treated as a crawler.\n\n## It is not the three-line fix it looks like\n\n`SiteScope.require(path, language, anyOf)` resolves the site that **owns\na path**. Neither GET had a path — they took no parameters at all — so\nthere was nothing to hand it.\n\nGating them is therefore a contract change. Both GETs now require `path`\nand `language` on the query string, exactly as the POST handlers already\nrequire them in their JSON body, and the three front-end call sites send\nthem. The returned values stay global to the module: the site is what\nthe caller must hold `publish` **on**, not what is looked up.\n\n## Where the gate lives\n\nIn `GeoServlet`, as `requireDashboard` — not pasted into both servlets.\nThis would have been the **third** copy of resolve-site-or-deny, and\nthat base class exists precisely because four copies of seven helpers\ndrifted apart and hid two real defects between them.\n\n`isGuest()` is removed. It had exactly one caller, which this PR\nchanges, and leaving the wrong-question helper sitting in the shared\nbase is how the same defect comes back. `requireUser` is what the POST\npaths already use. The bundle declares no `Export-Package`, so nothing\noutside it could call `isGuest`.\n\n## Why it survived three releases\n\n> **Correction.** An earlier version of this description said *\"nothing\never tested a GET\"*. That was wrong, and CI caught it. `report.spec.ts`\ncalled `GET /report` three times, directly through `cy.request` rather\nthan the `geoPost` helper — which is why grepping the helper missed\nthem. `crawler-check`''s GET really was never called.\n\nThe truth is worse than no coverage. One of those three tests was:\n\n```ts\nit(''may read the status'', () => {\n    cy.request({url: ENDPOINTS.report, ...}).then(response => {\n        expectJsonOk(response, ''the status only says whether a provider exists'');\n    });\n});\n```\n\nA contributor — `publication-start` but not `publish` — reading the\nstatus, **asserted as correct and passing on every run**. The defect was\nnot uncovered; it was *pinned as intended behaviour*.\n\nIts stated reason is wrong on its own terms: \"the status only says\nwhether a provider exists\" describes a body that also carries `provider`\nand `model`, and the assertion eleven lines above it spells out all\nthree keys. The test was written from what the endpoint did rather than\nfrom what it should do — which is how a characterisation test quietly\nbecomes a specification.\n\nThat test now asserts `403` and that the body carries no provider.\n\n## The new coverage\n\n`geoGet` is added, and `authorization.spec.ts` walks **both** GETs for\nthree actors:\n\n| actor | expected | why it is in the suite |\n|---|---|---|\n| guest | 401 | was already correct — proves the gate is not simply off\n|\n| **contributor** | **403** | **the regression test.** Holds\n`publication-start` but not `publish` |\n| publisher | 200 | without it, every refusal is consistent with a gate\nthat refuses everybody |\n\nThe middle row is the point. These endpoints were never open to\nanonymous callers, so a suite checking only guest-refused and\npublisher-allowed would have stayed green throughout the defect''s life.\nOnly an authenticated-but-unprivileged actor separates authentication\nfrom authorisation — and the fixture already had one. Each refusal also\nasserts the payload field is **absent**, because the discriminator here\nis the response body, not the status.\n\nA fourth case asserts `400 path required` when no site is named, and a\nnew guest case sends **no** query string at all and still expects 401 —\npinning the order, so an anonymous caller cannot tell \"malformed path\"\nfrom \"not yours\" and use the pair to discover which sites exist.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `mvn clean install` — **354 unit tests green**\n- [x] `tsc --noEmit` — no errors in `authorization.spec.ts`,\n`report.spec.ts` or `support/geo.ts`\n- [x] **Cypress e2e run locally in full** via `ci.build.sh` +\n`ci.startup.sh`\n\n## Notes for the reviewer\n\n- **`crawlerCheck.getStatus()` has no importer** anywhere in the product\n— the `crawler-check` GET has no front-end caller at all. It is updated\nfor consistency rather than deleted, since removing it is a separate\ndecision.\n- `tsc --noEmit` reports **9 pre-existing** implicit-`any` errors in\n`scan-outputs.spec.ts` and `visibility.spec.ts`, untouched here. No\nworkflow runs `typecheck`, so they are latent rather than blocking.\nHappy to clear them in their own PR.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signatu