pick up.\nThis writes the entry from the commits themselves.\n\nNo production code changes here.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"060c5fb4885356b76b9d05962551b7236194defc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/060c5fb4885356b76b9d05962551b7236194defc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/51eec32a38b29dd69e4c3d0f7c0a6256e5a3accf","verification":{"payload":"tree 060c5fb4885356b76b9d05962551b7236194defc\nparent 8fe51603fbea7f54c79a29a7787b7aea23304645\nauthor Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789571650 +0200\ncommitter Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789571650 +0200\n\nchore: release geo-readiness @ v1.3.2 (patch)\n\nRecords 1.3.2 in the changelog and moves the version chachalog tracks.\n\nThe two commits since 1.3.1 are both fixes and neither shipped a\nchangelog fragment, so there was nothing for the release PR to pick up.\nThis writes the entry from the commits themselves.\n\nNo production code changes here.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQEzBAABCgAdFiEEtcDrrZ6KblczSEt9UhsGUDwSL3kFAmqqskIACgkQUhsGUDwS\nL3k1aggAjMV/F1wCF96oHaUgYlw1VfwQukdgPStU+A/VuvO5134AdeQeNjoA9USO\nxiRTuUkpwpDMpit4rpnTijxoeU3UHG45xi5EjuLC0EUXmQSBLsCyJhai4lo13i85\n11QsCA4V0U5939tp3dqf2iZ16Znz1L8lvq0bumlcKCmUIZKr7c0BZ6dpPL8e8wyM\nGK0hsIrWwig5adeTLjy/lQH4M8j/f6mp4Hp59Y7jq/hFsb3noKiaH6jiRtRJjaNR\n/xwiQP/xRp6+e3WY/NGY5Ej98ktRY3sHNIoviFMI8P71mi7ABiED02EI+k8YQqg5\nRR0Yh0hsHHhjAxVJex9uoK4uJxQidw==\n=UcQz\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T15:14:16Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/51eec32a38b29dd69e4c3d0f7c0a6256e5a3accf","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDUxZWVjMzJhMzhiMjlkZDY5ZTRjM2QwZjdjMGE2MjU2ZTVhM2FjY2Y","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/8fe51603fbea7f54c79a29a7787b7aea23304645","sha":"8fe51603fbea7f54c79a29a7787b7aea23304645","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/8fe51603fbea7f54c79a29a7787b7aea23304645"}],"sha":"51eec32a38b29dd69e4c3d0f7c0a6256e5a3accf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/51eec32a38b29dd69e4c3d0f7c0a6256e5a3accf"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/722535?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/smonier","id":722535,"login":"smonier","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcyMjUzNQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/8fe51603fbea7f54c79a29a7787b7aea23304645/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T15:06:56Z","email":"smonier@Jahia.com","name":"Stephane Monier"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T15:06:56Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: clear the issues holding the quality gate red on main (#25)\n\n`main`''s quality gate fails three conditions, and has since before 1.3.1\nwas prepared. Re-measured this morning on `0bbbdcb`:\n\n| Condition | Value | Required |\n|---|---|---|\n| Added Technical Debt | 55 min | 0 |\n| Reliability Rating | C | A |\n| New Maintainability Issues | 8 | 0 |\n\nNone of these could have failed a pull request''s own gate. A PR gate\nmeasures that PR''s diff; the branch''s new-code period covers everything\nsince the previous version, so an issue Sonar attributes to an unchanged\nline of a changed file counts on `main` and nowhere else. #21 was an\nattempt at the same thing from the outside - it removed ten real unused\nimports and took the count from 10 to 8, but it was chosen by reading\nthe diff rather than the issue list. This one is the issue list.\n\n## The eight maintainability issues\n\n**`java:S1068`** - `PageFetch` still declared `JSONLD_TYPE` after #22\nstopped reading JSON-LD types with a regex and started parsing them.\nDead since that merge.\n\n**`java:S3398` x6** - a private helper used only by a nested class\nbelongs in it. One in `GeoScore` (`controlHtml` to `Facts`), one in\n`LinkGraph` (`row` to `Findings`), four in `SiteScorer` (to `Run`).\n\nThe four in `SiteScorer` are the root of a tree: `scorePage` calls\n`resolveUrl`, `fetch`, `recordCanonical`, `drawerReport` and\n`summarise`, those call `title`, `template`, `agentUa`, `pathOf` and\n`contradicts`, and `baseFor` calls `withoutTrailingSlashes`. Moving only\nthe four flagged would have made the next layer \"used only by `Run`\" and\nflagged it at the next analysis, and the layer after that at the one\nafter - a gate that goes red once per release for three releases. All\nfifteen move at once. They are the only callers of each other and `scan`\ndoes nothing but construct `Run`, so `Run` is what they serve.\n\nThe move is mechanical: sorting the stripped lines of the file before\nand after gives identical multisets, 612 each, nothing added and nothing\nremoved.\n\n**`java:S107`** - `Snapshot`''s constructor took thirteen parameters. It\nnow takes the configuration map and reads each field with its own\ndefault. That is also what lets `defaults()` be an empty map instead of\na second list of the same thirteen values, which had to be kept in step\nby hand and silently disagreed the moment one of them changed.\n\n## The reliability rating\n\nFive open issues, all front-end, and the four `MEDIUM` ones are what\nmakes the rating C:\n\n**`javascript:S6848` x4** - `Charts.jsx` has four plain boxes that carry\n`onMouseEnter`/`onMouseLeave` for the pointer tooltip. The accessibility\nwork in #14 took the tab stops off them and moved the numbers into the\nlist and table structures around them, which is the right shape, but\nleft the boxes with no role - so the rule still reads them as\ninteractive elements that a keyboard cannot reach. `role=\"none\"` says\nwhat is true: the box draws, and the text inside it is what gets read.\nIt hides nothing - the value spans are children and stay in the\naccessibility tree.\n\nThe two sites that already read as `<th>`/`<td>` are not flagged, which\nis the same rule agreeing that a native element needs no role.\n\n**`javascript:S7758`** - `languageFlag` read a code unit where it meant\na code point. Same answer for A-Z, wrong premise.\n\n## Also in here\n\n`SiteScopeTest` wrote two of its control-character inputs as raw bytes -\na literal NUL and a 0x01 inside `@ValueSource` - where the neighbours\nwere properly escaped. Git classifies the file as binary because of\nthem, which is how four kilobytes of tests merged in #16 with no diff\nanyone could read, and how any later change to them would have merged\ntoo. They are escapes now: same two strings, same four cases passing,\nplain text file.\n\n## What this PR cannot prove\n\nIts own gate will pass, but that is not evidence. It measures this diff,\nand the conditions that are failing are measured against `main`. The\nproof is the next analysis of `main` after this merges, which I will\ntrigger and report.\n\nBehaviour is unchanged. `mvn clean install -DskipTests=false` runs 354\nunit tests green locally.\n\nCo-authored-by: Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"0ccc8ea1711adac81ed585c339d787ccfd12d9b3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/0ccc8ea1711adac81ed585c339d787ccfd12d9b3"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/8fe51603fbea7f54c79a29a7787b7aea23304645","verification":{"payload":"tree 0ccc8ea1711adac81ed585c339d787ccfd12d9b3\nparent 86257a900725374649fac01af4301a8f969cdc3d\nauthor Stephane Monier <smonier@Jahia.com> 1789571216 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789571216 +0200\n\nfix: clear the issues holding the quality gate red on main (#25)\n\n`main`''s quality gate fails three conditions, and has since before 1.3.1\nwas prepared. Re-measured this morning on `0bbbdcb`:\n\n| Condition | Value | Required |\n|---|---|---|\n| Added Technical Debt | 55 min | 0 |\n| Reliability Rating | C | A |\n| New Maintainability Issues | 8 | 0 |\n\nNone of these could have failed a pull request''s own gate. A PR gate\nmeasures that PR''s diff; the branch''s new-code period covers everything\nsince the previous version, so an issue Sonar attributes to an unchanged\nline of a changed file counts on `main` and nowhere else. #21 was an\nattempt at the same thing from the outside - it removed ten real unused\nimports and took the count from 10 to 8, but it was chosen by reading\nthe diff rather than the issue list. This one is the issue list.\n\n## The eight maintainability issues\n\n**`java:S1068`** - `PageFetch` still declared `JSONLD_TYPE` after #22\nstopped reading JSON-LD types with a regex and started parsing them.\nDead since that merge.\n\n**`java:S3398` x6** - a private helper used only by a nested class\nbelongs in it. One in `GeoScore` (`controlHtml` to `Facts`), one in\n`LinkGraph` (`row` to `Findings`), four in `SiteScorer` (to `Run`).\n\nThe four in `SiteScorer` are the root of a tree: `scorePage` calls\n`resolveUrl`, `fetch`, `recordCanonical`, `drawerReport` and\n`summarise`, those call `title`, `template`, `agentUa`, `pathOf` and\n`contradicts`, and `baseFor` calls `withoutTrailingSlashes`. Moving only\nthe four flagged would have made the next layer \"used only by `Run`\" and\nflagged it at the next analysis, and the layer after that at the one\nafter - a gate that goes red once per release for three releases. All\nfifteen move at once. They are the only callers of each other and `scan`\ndoes nothing but construct `Run`, so `Run` is what they serve.\n\nThe move is mechanical: sorting the stripped lines of the file before\nand after gives identical multisets, 612 each, nothing added and nothing\nremoved.\n\n**`java:S107`** - `Snapshot`''s constructor took thirteen parameters. It\nnow takes the configuration map and reads each field with its own\ndefault. That is also what lets `defaults()` be an empty map instead of\na second list of the same thirteen values, which had to be kept in step\nby hand and silently disagreed the moment one of them changed.\n\n## The reliability rating\n\nFive open issues, all front-end, and the four `MEDIUM` ones are what\nmakes the rating C:\n\n**`javascript:S6848` x4** - `Charts.jsx` has four plain boxes that carry\n`onMouseEnter`/`onMouseLeave` for the pointer tooltip. The accessibility\nwork in #14 took the tab stops off them and moved the numbers into the\nlist and table structures around them, which is the right shape, but\nleft the boxes with no role - so the rule still reads them as\ninteractive elements that a keyboard cannot reach. `role=\"none\"` says\nwhat is true: the box draws, and the text inside it is what gets read.\nIt hides nothing - the value spans are children and stay in the\naccessibility tree.\n\nThe two sites that already read as `<th>`/`<td>` are not flagged, which\nis the same rule agreeing that a native element needs no role.\n\n**`javascript:S7758`** - `languageFlag` read a code unit where it meant\na code point. Same answer for A-Z, wrong premise.\n\n## Also in here\n\n`SiteScopeTest` wrote two of its control-character inputs as raw bytes -\na literal NUL and a 0x01 inside `@ValueSource` - where the neighbours\nwere properly escaped. Git classifies the file as binary because of\nthem, which is how four kilobytes of tests merged in #16 with no diff\nanyone could read, and how any later change to them would have merged\ntoo. They are escapes now: same two strings, same four cases passing,\nplain text file.\n\n## What this PR cannot prove\n\nIts own gate will pass, but that is not evidence. It measures this diff,\nand the conditions that are failing are measured against `main`. The\nproof is the next analysis of `main` after this merges, which I will\ntrigger and report.\n\nBehaviour is unchanged. `mvn clean install -DskipTests=false` runs 354\nunit tests green locally.\n\nCo-authored-by: Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqrCQCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAASuUQAB31sm1diKkmUNwRKLvv7JDz\nKNx59CKCtRXGJJvWS4yfFJkHfHQC/wIygEk7SST83G1HDrjQEDGhOpV0IGXA3UU9\ngfsSGpZMtcQLmM0sGYZ0w92A/cT4mBOtEYLwL4eoi/vlBNLDB3MvniYpgjNF2SVI\nWh4/idkCwRxMgVMpc5lz9d4RsypEyKmYJ4/gnSDkPNpmWQwPnnrmcbnsGuz1t/RW\nFKrXkPlR13d1CX6ldmZRWhztf5HwFiPYJzklsaP9hUX3noNYGxEHMJAC8JDIILgA\nD81syuFRvCw3r5Gzd40fMDFNDCo/klcRoJlRpkqR8VRXBP2GRLp26/ZpukYAs7hq\n6j/l7sbf2/sQxDocknsWrs0LlZVgqurW71+h8RN2c1mj/F0mCRqbTmA4w8E/fIKN\nycMEC/W0jZSdYZvdCksLWCoW2T3e4augHxaVEWguNn38iuXBtKw1oQSDNHEmKIAs\nzwKmeIyvVdYDSpB+Im9+DnUkaB5Smo3JWw69y/m2nvrmSSfXXAy3ZbyYyG/2j2G7\nB7WJPBZUTp5CHWR37UyRcdkVmjRcUDBdXW1XJJtgMvt4aubLkp9NLFqqc1dGKm+y\nAjSYbWcy31m1evn1YpdvwkGzUeO0tS/sMqhLyxXiESALJcSD+6ArOYIJORTj0me9\n/sAsWbA/y3e3x0ZTVwP0\n=+3cS\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T15:06:56Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/8fe51603fbea7f54c79a29a7787b7aea23304645","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDhmZTUxNjAzZmJlYTdmNTRjNzlhMjlhNzc4N2I3YWVhMjMzMDQ2NDU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/86257a900725374649fac01af4301a8f969cdc3d","sha":"86257a900725374649fac01af4301a8f969cdc3d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/86257a900725374649fac01af4301a8f969cdc3d"}],"sha":"8fe51603fbea7f54c79a29a7787b7aea23304645","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/8fe51603fbea7f54c79a29a7787b7aea23304645"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/722535?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/smonier","id":722535,"login":"smonier","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcyMjUzNQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/86257a900725374649fac01af4301a8f969cdc3d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T14:47:48Z","email":"smonier@Jahia.com","name":"Stephane Monier"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T14:47:48Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: let the release action''s git commands run in the build container (#26)\n\nCutting 1.3.1 fired `Trigger Release creation` for the first time that\ngot past checkout, and it failed on the release action''s very first git\ncommand:\n\n```\nfatal: detected dubious ownership in repository at ''/__w/geo-readiness/geo-readiness''\n```\n\n`actions/checkout` writes the working copy as the runner user; every\nstep after it runs as root inside `jahia-docker-mvn-cache`, and git\nrefuses to operate on a repository owned by somebody else. The action''s\nfirst step is `git fetch --all`, so it dies before the version is set,\nbefore the tag, before anything is pushed - the failed run left the\nrepository untouched.\n\nDeclaring the workspace trusted is the standard remedy and the shared\naction does not do it for us: `jahia/jahia-modules-action/release@v2`\ngoes straight from `Prepare GPG` to `Getting latest changes from the\nremote branch`.\n\nThe 1.3.0 attempt on 14 September never reached this point - it failed\none step earlier, on the SSH checkout, with `Repository not found`,\nwhich is why 1.3.0''s commits on `main` are hand-made and its jar was\nattached by hand. That access has since been fixed.\n\n## This is not the only wall\n\nTwo repository rulesets will stop the same run further along, and\nneither can be fixed from a workflow file:\n\n**`Prevent tag deletion`** (target: tag, include `~ALL`, **no bypass\nactors**) vs the action''s own step:\n\n```\n- name: Delete github tag ${{ inputs.release_version }}\n  run: |\n    git tag --delete ${{ inputs.release_version }}\n    git push origin :refs/tags/${{ inputs.release_version }}\n```\n\nThe Jahia release flow deletes the tag GitHub created with the release\nand lets `mvn release:prepare` make it again. With no bypass actors,\nthat push is refused for everybody, including admins.\n\n**`support-baseline`** (target: default branch, rules `pull_request` +\n`required_signatures`, bypass: one human user) vs `release:prepare`,\nwhich pushes the \"prepare release\" and \"next development version\"\ncommits straight to `main`, unsigned - this workflow does not pass\n`sign_commits`, so the action''s `Prepare GPG` step is skipped. Both\nrules refuse that push.\n\nSo this PR is necessary but not sufficient. Getting a CI release to\ncomplete also needs the CI identity added as a bypass actor on both\nrulesets - or the release kept as a manual job, which is what 1.3.0 was.","tree":{"sha":"93618076091a176f5c5697ec3f1132d8fb1f007e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/93618076091a176f5c5697ec3f1132d8fb1f007e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/86257a900725374649fac01af4301a8f969cdc3d","verification":{"payload":"tree 93618076091a176f5c5697ec3f1132d8fb1f007e\nparent 0bbbdcb4921d0e89d8f88e1d716dfe4d4791c689\nauthor Stephane Monier <smonier@Jahia.com> 1789570068 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789570068 +0200\n\nfix: let the release action''s git commands run in the build container (#26)\n\nCutting 1.3.1 fired `Trigger Release creation` for the first time that\ngot past checkout, and it failed on the release action''s very first git\ncommand:\n\n```\nfatal: detected dubious ownership in repository at ''/__w/geo-readiness/geo-readiness''\n```\n\n`actions/checkout` writes the working copy as the runner user; every\nstep after it runs as root inside `jahia-docker-mvn-cache`, and git\nrefuses to operate on a repository owned by somebody else. The action''s\nfirst step is `git fetch --all`, so it dies before the version is set,\nbefore the tag, before anything is pushed - the failed run left the\nrepository untouched.\n\nDeclaring the workspace trusted is the standard remedy and the shared\naction does not do it for us: `jahia/jahia-modules-action/release@v2`\ngoes straight from `Prepare GPG` to `Getting latest changes from the\nremote branch`.\n\nThe 1.3.0 attempt on 14 September never reached this point - it failed\none step earlier, on the SSH checkout, with `Repository not found`,\nwhich is why 1.3.0''s commits on `main` are hand-made and its jar was\nattached by hand. That access has since been fixed.\n\n## This is not the only wall\n\nTwo repository rulesets will stop the same run further along, and\nneither can be fixed from a workflow file:\n\n**`Prevent tag deletion`** (target: tag, include `~ALL`, **no bypass\nactors**) vs the action''s own step:\n\n```\n- name: Delete github tag ${{ inputs.release_version }}\n  run: |\n    git tag --delete ${{ inputs.release_version }}\n    git push origin :refs/tags/${{ inputs.release_version }}\n```\n\nThe Jahia release flow deletes the tag GitHub created with the release\nand lets `mvn release:prepare` make it again. With no bypass actors,\nthat push is refused for everybody, including admins.\n\n**`support-baseline`** (target: default branch, rules `pull_request` +\n`required_signatures`, bypass: one human user) vs `release:prepare`,\nwhich pushes the \"prepare release\" and \"next development version\"\ncommits straight to `main`, unsigned - this workflow does not pass\n`sign_commits`, so the action''s `Prepare GPG` step is skipped. Both\nrules refuse that push.\n\nSo this PR is necessary but not sufficient. Getting a CI release to\ncomplete also needs the CI identity added as a bypass actor on both\nrulesets - or the release kept as a manual job, which is what 1.3.0 was.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqqwUCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAMQQQAHHhvpiuR/VYwHblYKl+dhHT\nSydVQ9MyPjTXGfsIV5MP+dO59tRX4Vy5yOQBqat4nexqgLO9WOJv8w+2/JMsN8Oo\nUg/aaMlvN0Zg5fLbuuglmv499eMKi1qrFfwFFvgJvR0GXz0+gb4ID4PKgooCe0R6\nj+0IUXmhprSSuPjEpRT2V6dYhyDWAb7ys0At2lOn9k8JTQi0CYljMYQsPxCNtw8+\nayEHBbTDpf5I50Yjxg1uFq6yyYxWNlc8eIBsWx8YwUnSMH/LmB6u594pt+JkR8g4\n6pdJhd11N5S/sIQPMUvYJOkZJoDCOYOAEXpBuXFRt2VS2GIPdb0LHwbbB3Qa6TV2\nuHiVDpiq76o7cW+4SykIGA7VbBU8VS1A7qNM04xKn29xxGk/OPGA2EBDXuXVdsNq\n/EFDX4FL7zeJeQJc5U1k/TKfX6+Ey+PqQBKfj8Rprmhk48HWEML2XO3TNin4GoJu\ntR7jAoFjwAEUnZDN6T8uoQV3wmoZipW38qky0Dss/3g1S+Ljx8S1EVZI8/UdlUu8\nNPvIzRiP824juIIc9+BHWZmm3aTHBNiQeFekw2jJP7RpUkvnFF6EXgfuN3/Q3L7I\n4XsFjUvxPNfYiDkowJN98FHEDXh6CdD8Gihngh1whAbXdctEKw6Vq1inx0GtDjTN\nssyp++jfgM8hn7w8trUc\n=D6DS\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T14:47:49Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/86257a900725374649fac01af4301a8f969cdc3d","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDg2MjU3YTkwMDcyNTM3NDY0OWZhYzAxYWY0MzAxYThmOTY5Y2RjM2Q","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/0bbbdcb4921d0e89d8f88e1d716dfe4d4791c689","sha":"0bbbdcb4921d0e89d8f88e1d716dfe4d4791c689","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/0bbbdcb4921d0e89d8f88e1d716dfe4d4791c689"}],"sha":"86257a900725374649fac01af4301a8f969cdc3d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/86257a900725374649fac01af4301a8f969cdc3d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/15368?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/github-actions","id":41898282,"login":"github-actions[bot]","node_id":"MDM6Qm90NDE4OTgyODI=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","type":"Bot","url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/0bbbdcb4921d0e89d8f88e1d716dfe4d4791c689/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T11:12:13Z","email":"41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com","name":"github-actions[bot]"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T11:12:13Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"chore: release geo-readiness @ v1.3.1 (patch) (#8)\n\n## [🦜](https://github.com/GauBen/chachalog) Chachalog\n\nThis PR will bump the following packages:\n\n<details><summary><code>geo-readiness</code> 1.3.0 → 1.3.1\n(patch)</summary>\n\n> * The dashboard and the drawer now meet WCAG 2.2 AAA on contrast,\ncarry real headings, and announce a running scan to a screen reader. The\ncharts stopped making every data mark a tab stop — a full failure matrix\nwas upwards of three hundred of them — and instead carry their numbers\nas text inside the table and list structures they already had, so\nassistive tech reads the figures and the keyboard passes through in one\nstop. The drawer is a real `<dialog>`, and every control that had no\naccessible name has one.\n>\n> * The public base url now takes its host from the repository rather\nthan from the request that matched it. The two were equal under a\ncase-insensitive comparison but not identical, so a `Host:` header could\nchoose the casing of a base url that the scan servlet then persisted in\n`geoBaseUrl` and reused on every later scheduled scan.\n>\n> * Sped up reading of fetched pages, sitemaps and site files, which\ncould take minutes on some documents.\n>\n> * Moved the module to the `org.jahia.community` groupId. The Java\npackage, the OSGi configuration file and the bundle name are unchanged,\nso an existing install upgrades in place and keeps its settings.\n>\n> * The runtime configuration is swapped through an AtomicReference, so\nthe operation that replaces it reads as the atomic one it always had to\nbe.\n>\n> * Decomposed the four methods Sonar scored furthest over the\ncognitive-complexity limit — the scoring engine at 43, the robots.txt\nrule evaluator at 27, the llms.txt freshness comparison at 19 and the\nrobots.txt parser at 16. Behaviour is unchanged, and the\ncharacterisation tests added in the previous release are what proves it.\n>\n> * Decomposed four more methods over the cognitive-complexity limit —\nthe HTML analyser at 26, the link-graph report at 28, the robots.txt\neditor''s parser at 16 and the guest-visibility classifier — each behind\ncharacterisation tests written first. Unit coverage of the module\nroughly doubled, and nine classes are now at or near complete coverage.\n>\n> * Audited every factual claim in the documentation against the code\nand corrected what was wrong, including two instructions that would have\nbroken a contributor''s setup: a `yarn e2e` command that does not exist,\nand a CI `instance_type` the live workflow deliberately omits because it\nfails. Two of the corrections were code rather than prose — the test\nharness dropped the `notests` flag it documented, and a comment claimed\nthe module refuses to fetch a site with no server name when it in fact\nfalls back to `localhost:8080`.\n>\n> * Fixed crawler and site scans so repeated checks remain reliable and\nsitemap comparisons no longer follow links to other sites.\n>\n> * Fixed the `$` end-anchor in robots.txt matching, which was wrong in\ntwo opposite directions: a pattern with no wildcard checked its start\nand its end independently, so `/foo$` matched `/foo/bar/foo`, and a\npattern ending in `*$` rejected every path longer than its literal\nprefix, so `abc*$` did not match `abcdef`. Sites whose robots.txt uses\n`$` anchors will see corrected verdicts in the drawer''s policy\ncross-check and may see their score move.\n>\n> * The language, freshness, sitemap and schema checks are now covered\nby tests that assert what they report rather than that they answered,\nand the three methods behind them have been decomposed to sit under the\ncomplexity limit.\n>\n> * Closed an SSRF where the request''s Host header chose the port this\nmodule connected to, gated the one endpoint that had no permission\ncheck, stopped a browsing node deleting every site''s scan schedule from\nthe cluster, and fixed six places where the code did the opposite of its\nown comment - including a config marker typo that silently reset the\nadministrator''s settings on every redeploy.\n>\n> * Gave the four servlets one base class instead of four copies of the\nsame seven helpers, which fixed two defects that were living in the\ndrift between them: three of the module''s four JSON endpoints were\nserved without `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` while echoing content\nread from the site being checked, and three of them silently truncated\nan oversized request body so it came back as \"malformed body\" instead of\nbeing refused for what it was.\n>\n> * Nothing escapes a servlet method any more, the servlets hold no\nmutable shared state, an interrupted crawler check no longer swallows\nthe interrupt, and the language pattern is bounded so a long input\ncannot overflow the matcher''s stack.\n>\n> * Fixed seven defects the new characterisation tests had pinned. The\nlargest: a page whose JSON-LD is syntactically broken now correctly\nreports no structured data, where it used to pass the check because the\ntypes were read out of the text with a regex rather than parsed. The\nrobots.txt editor also now keeps the promise it makes in its own\ndocumentation — a merge that changes nothing returns a customer''s file\nexactly as it arrived, rather than dropping blank lines, adding a\ntrailing newline or mixing line endings into it.\n>\n> * The module has unit tests for the first time: JUnit 5, AssertJ and\nJaCoCo, with 189 characterisation tests pinning the scoring engine, the\nrobots.txt matcher, the llms.txt freshness comparison and the two\nsecurity guards. They exist so the decomposition that follows cannot\nchange a score silently — the existing Cypress suite asserts that a scan\nhappened and that the UI reads \"n of m checks passed\", never which n or\nwhich m.\n>\n> * Removed ten imports left behind by the decomposition, which were the\nlast thing keeping the quality gate red on the main branch.\n\n</details>\n\nCo-authored-by: github-actions[bot] <41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"77c23c0ca9e4cd48c3137d573d7a97aa54a4569c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/77c23c0ca9e4cd48c3137d573d7a97aa54a4569c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/0bbbdcb4921d0e89d8f88e1d716dfe4d4791c689","verification":{"payload":"tree 77c23c0ca9e4cd48c3137d573d7a97aa54a4569c\nparent 6315bf147108122dca60afa54db7d00a3547b978\nauthor github-actions[bot] <41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com> 1789557133 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789557133 +0200\n\nchore: release geo-readiness @ v1.3.1 (patch) (#8)\n\n## [🦜](https://github.com/GauBen/chachalog) Chachalog\n\nThis PR will bump the following packages:\n\n<details><summary><code>geo-readiness</code> 1.3.0 → 1.3.1\n(patch)</summary>\n\n> * The dashboard and the drawer now meet WCAG 2.2 AAA on contrast,\ncarry real headings, and announce a running scan to a screen reader. The\ncharts stopped making every data mark a tab stop — a full failure matrix\nwas upwards of three hundred of them — and instead carry their numbers\nas text inside the table and list structures they already had, so\nassistive tech reads the figures and the keyboard passes through in one\nstop. The drawer is a real `<dialog>`, and every control that had no\naccessible name has one.\n>\n> * The public base url now takes its host from the repository rather\nthan from the request that matched it. The two were equal under a\ncase-insensitive comparison but not identical, so a `Host:` header could\nchoose the casing of a base url that the scan servlet then persisted in\n`geoBaseUrl` and reused on every later scheduled scan.\n>\n> * Sped up reading of fetched pages, sitemaps and site files, which\ncould take minutes on some documents.\n>\n> * Moved the module to the `org.jahia.community` groupId. The Java\npackage, the OSGi configuration file and the bundle name are unchanged,\nso an existing install upgrades in place and keeps its settings.\n>\n> * The runtime configuration is swapped through an AtomicReference, so\nthe operation that replaces it reads as the atomic one it always had to\nbe.\n>\n> * Decomposed the four methods Sonar scored furthest over the\ncognitive-complexity limit — the scoring engine at 43, the robots.txt\nrule evaluator at 27, the llms.txt freshness comparison at 19 and the\nrobots.txt parser at 16. Behaviour is unchanged, and the\ncharacterisation tests added in the previous release are what proves it.\n>\n> * Decomposed four more methods over the cognitive-complexity limit —\nthe HTML analyser at 26, the link-graph report at 28, the robots.txt\neditor''s parser at 16 and the guest-visibility classifier — each behind\ncharacterisation tests written first. Unit coverage of the module\nroughly doubled, and nine classes are now at or near complete coverage.\n>\n> * Audited every factual claim in the documentation against the code\nand corrected what was wrong, including two instructions that would have\nbroken a contributor''s setup: a `yarn e2e` command that does not exist,\nand a CI `instance_type` the live workflow deliberately omits because it\nfails. Two of the corrections were code rather than prose — the test\nharness dropped the `notests` flag it documented, and a comment claimed\nthe module refuses to fetch a site with no server name when it in fact\nfalls back to `localhost:8080`.\n>\n> * Fixed crawler and site scans so repeated checks remain reliable and\nsitemap comparisons no longer follow links to other sites.\n>\n> * Fixed the `$` end-anchor in robots.txt matching, which was wrong in\ntwo opposite directions: a pattern with no wildcard checked its start\nand its end independently, so `/foo$` matched `/foo/bar/foo`, and a\npattern ending in `*$` rejected every path longer than its literal\nprefix, so `abc*$` did not match `abcdef`. Sites whose robots.txt uses\n`$` anchors will see corrected verdicts in the drawer''s policy\ncross-check and may see their score move.\n>\n> * The language, freshness, sitemap and schema checks are now covered\nby tests that assert what they report rather than that they answered,\nand the three methods behind them have been decomposed to sit under the\ncomplexity limit.\n>\n> * Closed an SSRF where the request''s Host header chose the port this\nmodule connected to, gated the one endpoint that had no permission\ncheck, stopped a browsing node deleting every site''s scan schedule from\nthe cluster, and fixed six places where the code did the opposite of its\nown comment - including a config marker typo that silently reset the\nadministrator''s settings on every redeploy.\n>\n> * Gave the four servlets one base class instead of four copies of the\nsame seven helpers, which fixed two defects that were living in the\ndrift between them: three of the module''s four JSON endpoints were\nserved without `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` while echoing content\nread from the site being checked, and three of them silently truncated\nan oversized request body so it came back as \"malformed body\" instead of\nbeing refused for what it was.\n>\n> * Nothing escapes a servlet method any more, the servlets hold no\nmutable shared state, an interrupted crawler check no longer swallows\nthe interrupt, and the language pattern is bounded so a long input\ncannot overflow the matcher''s stack.\n>\n> * Fixed seven defects the new characterisation tests had pinned. The\nlargest: a page whose JSON-LD is syntactically broken now correctly\nreports no structured data, where it used to pass the check because the\ntypes were read out of the text with a regex rather than parsed. The\nrobots.txt editor also now keeps the promise it makes in its own\ndocumentation — a merge that changes nothing returns a customer''s file\nexactly as it arrived, rather than dropping blank lines, adding a\ntrailing newline or mixing line endings into it.\n>\n> * The module has unit tests for the first time: JUnit 5, AssertJ and\nJaCoCo, with 189 characterisation tests pinning the scoring engine, the\nrobots.txt matcher, the llms.txt freshness comparison and the two\nsecurity guards. They exist so the decomposition that follows cannot\nchange a score silently — the existing Cypress suite asserts that a scan\nhappened and that the UI reads \"n of m checks passed\", never which n or\nwhich m.\n>\n> * Removed ten imports left behind by the decomposition, which were the\nlast thing keeping the quality gate red on the main branch.\n\n</details>\n\nCo-authored-by: github-actions[bot] <41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqnmNCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA3wwQAH7iZmIoJD6qupyVYSUMshji\nGUHTAz/y55xd1VMyVerLdySFU7L/X6mm4OwXvo8GpjaJVm3LpMa6fjjNOJa3xqoK\nNbNjOOr49OpsfLADy6130zRbqLFxi7mwBYUlFfDNKKSfiQJb0EbdH6wS+iLpOerj\nM1I6b8PHMRhWKMr0/o5cCTdKWa1ut9wF8LFfNUQf6R9q2ylhz/oPo/zN66yuGYWE\nV2NBeHlyBldZxqH40LJ7LodoUYlRl9F/3Rh1g55gU0mtzfHgwLkk+2DqFSyhbvcP\ngjIYwsIul2k3HQhRpaLnEKJmjVic/8a2ZdDZV/YjMnjfZodv94iVRks+WY9Uv5TZ\nqR6iF/ZG/UTLq0C45+tZ+6r62dgMj9fZ8MIe86zq/csxn38obM9I6OsdmPHPlYvY\nn/JoJwRHkMGs6fa0EWx8ISsNOEygvbx67pJuSnLSsq+ZRO8AXfuN1u1YtrPcUHVn\niOjZ7ymzVxVhCcX9S5KgWXxP0o79OMauGmI7I65jDR5BM+yYViQR9B/l+V+aO1gt\neRGenKC0rhHp5klWlKsY59P4G5su1Rpu70g9tk3Nuan+5cc01ec3kYGyuFpGUOSm\n1S8saPqUfV9RL01JgBHL0HZAxHEeW/GavNzxrPy+zhmGtkxI2Oa71hLYZE2Pvy64\nA/RdwnHX3m3HLnC7Nl8i\n=XwvF\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T11:12:14Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/0bbbdcb4921d0e89d8f88e1d716dfe4d4791c689","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDBiYmJkY2I0OTIxZDBlODlkOGY4OGUxZDcxNmRmZTRkNDc5MWM2ODk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/6315bf147108122dca60afa54db7d00a3547b978","sha":"6315bf147108122dca60afa54db7d00a3547b978","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/6315bf147108122dca60afa54db7d00a3547b978"}],"sha":"0bbbdcb4921d0e89d8f88e1d716dfe4d4791c689","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/0bbbdcb4921d0e89d8f88e1d716dfe4d4791c689"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/6315bf147108122dca60afa54db7d00a3547b978/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T10:16:28Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T10:16:28Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test: assert what the repository-backed scans report, then decompose them (#24)\n\nTests first, then the decomposition they make safe — and one method\ndeliberately left alone.\n\n## The coverage these four actually had\n\n`languages` was called **once** in the whole suite, inside\n`rate-limit.spec.ts`, as an example of an action that is *not* metered.\nThat call asserts only that the endpoint answered 200. `freshness`,\n`sitemap` and `schema` were **never called at all**.\n\nSo what was covered was the servlet''s plumbing — authentication, the\npermission gate, the shape of a refusal. Nothing touched what the checks\ncompute. `Languages.check` could have swapped `translated` and\n`missing`, telling a customer their French site is complete when it is\nempty, and the suite would have stayed green.\n\n## Fifteen assertions on the answer\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **languages** | the direction of `translated` vs `missing`, and\n`coverage` derived from the row''s own counts rather than asserted as a\nliteral — so the number the dashboard quotes has to be the one its\ncounts add up to |\n| **freshness** | the five bands **partition** the dated pages, and\nundated sorts **first** — the defect fixed in #13, which until now had\nno end-to-end assertion that would catch it returning |\n| **sitemap** | the early-return contract: three named reasons for\nhaving no usable sitemap, and the comparison keys **absent rather than\nzero**, because zero reads as \"the sitemap lists nothing\" |\n| **schema** | `complete ⊆ mapped ⊆ total`, and a saved mapping reads\nback |\n\nEvery block asserts a **premise** first. Without one, a fixture that\nfailed to build produces an empty scan and every later assertion passes\nby describing nothing.\n\n## One test exists only to record a limit\n\nWriting this found that `SitemapCheck` **returns before calling\n`PublishedMap.forSite`** when no sitemap is served — which is the case\nin this environment. So counting this spec as coverage of `forSite`\nwould have been wrong. `freshness` and `schema` are what exercise it\nhere, and there is now a test saying so, because the alternative is\nsomeone later reading \"sitemap spec exists\" as \"sitemap path is\ncovered\".\n\n## Then the decomposition\n\n| Method | Was | Now |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Languages.check` | 22 | `row`, `score`, `spread` |\n| `Languages.countBy` | 22 | `countInto`, `itemsOfType`,\n`countTranslations` |\n| `PublishedMap.forSite` | 18 | `collect`, `itemsOfType`, `record` |\n\nBoth count methods run the same two queries — `jnt:page` and\n`jmix:mainResource` — and a node can be both. Both already deduplicated,\nand neither said why: counting an item twice makes the total larger than\nthe site, every coverage percentage smaller than the truth, and\ndouble-counts the same page in the freshness distribution, the sitemap\ncomparison and the link graph. Written down now, beside the `Set` that\ndoes it.\n\n`countBy`''s deprecated `doExecuteWithSystemSession` is swapped for\n`...AsUser` — the same call by its current name. Only that one site; the\nmodule''s other uses are left alone so their pre-existing findings do not\nbecome this PR''s.\n\n## What is deliberately not here\n\n**`ScanStore.schedules`, complexity 17.** Its only caller is\n`ScanLifecycle`''s OSGi activation, so no HTTP request reaches it and no\nCypress spec can. Decomposing it would mean rewriting a method with\nnothing able to show the output had not moved — the mistake this\nsequence exists to avoid. It stays, by decision rather than oversight.\n\nThat takes the module from **14 methods over the complexity limit to\n1**.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **354 unit tests** pass\n- **148/148 Cypress** assertions pass — and for these three that is the\ncheck that counts, because no unit test reaches them\n\nSequenced after #13–#23.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"a57a690b050cc38af00d455a2c2e71ffaf552862","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/a57a690b050cc38af00d455a2c2e71ffaf552862"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/6315bf147108122dca60afa54db7d00a3547b978","verification":{"payload":"tree a57a690b050cc38af00d455a2c2e71ffaf552862\nparent e67a635d5c7bf12736d0a4561af7ce5b11f75e38\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789553788 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789553788 +0200\n\ntest: assert what the repository-backed scans report, then decompose them (#24)\n\nTests first, then the decomposition they make safe — and one method\ndeliberately left alone.\n\n## The coverage these four actually had\n\n`languages` was called **once** in the whole suite, inside\n`rate-limit.spec.ts`, as an example of an action that is *not* metered.\nThat call asserts only that the endpoint answered 200. `freshness`,\n`sitemap` and `schema` were **never called at all**.\n\nSo what was covered was the servlet''s plumbing — authentication, the\npermission gate, the shape of a refusal. Nothing touched what the checks\ncompute. `Languages.check` could have swapped `translated` and\n`missing`, telling a customer their French site is complete when it is\nempty, and the suite would have stayed green.\n\n## Fifteen assertions on the answer\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **languages** | the direction of `translated` vs `missing`, and\n`coverage` derived from the row''s own counts rather than asserted as a\nliteral — so the number the dashboard quotes has to be the one its\ncounts add up to |\n| **freshness** | the five bands **partition** the dated pages, and\nundated sorts **first** — the defect fixed in #13, which until now had\nno end-to-end assertion that would catch it returning |\n| **sitemap** | the early-return contract: three named reasons for\nhaving no usable sitemap, and the comparison keys **absent rather than\nzero**, because zero reads as \"the sitemap lists nothing\" |\n| **schema** | `complete ⊆ mapped ⊆ total`, and a saved mapping reads\nback |\n\nEvery block asserts a **premise** first. Without one, a fixture that\nfailed to build produces an empty scan and every later assertion passes\nby describing nothing.\n\n## One test exists only to record a limit\n\nWriting this found that `SitemapCheck` **returns before calling\n`PublishedMap.forSite`** when no sitemap is served — which is the case\nin this environment. So counting this spec as coverage of `forSite`\nwould have been wrong. `freshness` and `schema` are what exercise it\nhere, and there is now a test saying so, because the alternative is\nsomeone later reading \"sitemap spec exists\" as \"sitemap path is\ncovered\".\n\n## Then the decomposition\n\n| Method | Was | Now |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Languages.check` | 22 | `row`, `score`, `spread` |\n| `Languages.countBy` | 22 | `countInto`, `itemsOfType`,\n`countTranslations` |\n| `PublishedMap.forSite` | 18 | `collect`, `itemsOfType`, `record` |\n\nBoth count methods run the same two queries — `jnt:page` and\n`jmix:mainResource` — and a node can be both. Both already deduplicated,\nand neither said why: counting an item twice makes the total larger than\nthe site, every coverage percentage smaller than the truth, and\ndouble-counts the same page in the freshness distribution, the sitemap\ncomparison and the link graph. Written down now, beside the `Set` that\ndoes it.\n\n`countBy`''s deprecated `doExecuteWithSystemSession` is swapped for\n`...AsUser` — the same call by its current name. Only that one site; the\nmodule''s other uses are left alone so their pre-existing findings do not\nbecome this PR''s.\n\n## What is deliberately not here\n\n**`ScanStore.schedules`, complexity 17.** Its only caller is\n`ScanLifecycle`''s OSGi activation, so no HTTP request reaches it and no\nCypress spec can. Decomposing it would mean rewriting a method with\nnothing able to show the output had not moved — the mistake this\nsequence exists to avoid. It stays, by decision rather than oversight.\n\nThat takes the module from **14 methods over the complexity limit to\n1**.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **354 unit tests** pass\n- **148/148 Cypress** assertions pass — and for these three that is the\ncheck that counts, because no unit test reaches them\n\nSequenced after #13–#23.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqmx9CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAC4UQAEmSmusyPvjdpC/YDNBIk6ou\n/nyKmfD0QsMDpzQ9huxM8+k5TMJZfPAqUOEiEFRl6lfPNcIKdN3G/47zAgaQoaP9\ncBaytftLWCz6tuHvjJnw6sYhQMlojlVMBC8KjwSbUQqkme+YBJ8K7nR82tAPup7z\nK3nWso80sX+kAYv0Kpqm/m9BC7zYYKXahAAlOONEkSl64EE1g7ytM2ze1k4QTTIj\nONq8PvaAPkYDwJbONx6dfuqHOzy8/Vlysr/MYKhP70cQlsWyOM3LoVvMbXQrsnEM\n61QVREZZbagEe7F9jFk36EYdgjTmW5uiSlpWtegxfJNSYJW9pLywmrgdhtgO/BQ4\nxjzJMc1k9BS/02H6ejNo8Um7T05SF1M3y/z6H/16gUefbR1XuixNbjzi4JQVIB8C\n/wmUCMcQhe5/SpdKy5o65rnCMlPmk/J+HFiIHqHEQvK7daSdiEWPKysPaLHrUFs6\n6mh1izoEgebs3uxVCzCVPlGcArGPvrj378RiT3A/7lSxVoHE4t4zVehyuH00vo1E\ndAcR7/dwfCq7RXDAQ9bPRGxPbddbsW4aL8BTopDx+gWSNMu3r+2Og6emdSYVg9eC\nYJpsUneeRkZQIJutcO+YB/OF8owbM5WFk88J3kQATtXH8SZneI35h+pw0EffMCoS\nbZiF6am15nHTKJ680Z8m\n=phzl\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T10:16:29Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/6315bf147108122dca60afa54db7d00a3547b978","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDYzMTViZjE0NzEwODEyMmRjYTYwYWZhNTRkYjdkMDBhMzU0N2I5Nzg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/e67a635d5c7bf12736d0a4561af7ce5b11f75e38","sha":"e67a635d5c7bf12736d0a4561af7ce5b11f75e38","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/e67a635d5c7bf12736d0a4561af7ce5b11f75e38"}],"sha":"6315bf147108122dca60afa54db7d00a3547b978","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/6315bf147108122dca60afa54db7d00a3547b978"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/e67a635d5c7bf12736d0a4561af7ce5b11f75e38/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T09:08:32Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T09:08:32Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test: assert what the visibility check reports, and correct the docs it contradicts (#23)\n\nTwo things at once, because they turned out to be the same thing: the\nCypress suite now asserts what a check **reports**, and the\ndocumentation now says what the code **does**.\n\n## The e2e suite asserted almost no results\n\n127 assertions, and essentially all of them about refusals — who gets\n403, what gets 400, which paths are rejected. The happy path proved a\nscan *happened*: `has(''run'')`, `startedAt` not null.\n\n`guestVisibility` was called three times in the whole suite, **all\ninside `rate-limit.spec.ts`**, to spend the per-user budget. Nothing\nasserted what it returns. Inverting `isolatedRestriction` and\n`gatedBranch` — telling a customer their members area is a\nmisconfiguration, or their misconfiguration is a members area — would\nnot have failed anything.\n\n### What the fixture now builds\n\n```\nhome                 readable\n  geo-gated          ACL inheritance broken  →  isolatedRestriction  (findings)\n    geo-gated-child  inherits the closure    →  gatedBranch          (deliberate)\n```\n\nTwo pages, not one, because **the distinction is the check''s whole\noutput**. A closed page under an open one is the permission somebody\nforgot; under a closed one it is a members area working as intended.\nReporting the second as a defect is how a check gets ignored.\n\nThis cannot be a unit test. In Jahia a server administrator is a\n**role**, and a role arrives through ACL entries — exactly what breaking\ninheritance removes, so after the break every account but `root` and the\nsystem session is denied. And the check reads `live` through a real\nguest session precisely because a system session bypasses the ACLs it is\nmeasuring. Mocking that session mocks the thing under test.\n\nOne assertion guards the rest: `scanned > 0`. If the fixture silently\nfails to build, every other assertion would pass by describing an empty\nscan.\n\n**133/133**, up from 127.\n\n## The documentation audit found two CODE defects\n\nThe prose was right and the code was not — the shape this module keeps\nproducing.\n\n**`ci.startup.sh notests` never worked.** The header says *\"Pass\n`notests` to boot Jahia only\"*; the last line called the package''s\n`ci.startup` with no `\"$@\"`, so the flag was read off this script''s\narguments and never handed on. The documented \"boot Jahia only\" step\nbooted the stack **and ran every spec**. Forwarded in all four wrappers.\nI wrote that script in #12.\n\n**A comment misled the README.** `PublicUrls.derived()` said *\"the site\ndoes not say where it lives, so neither can we … `PUBLIC_BASE_URL` is\nthe way to say it\"* — then returned `http://localhost:8080` and fetched\nit. On a box serving several sites that is the **default** site, so the\ncheck reports another site''s pages and robots.txt as if they were yours,\nquietly, because the fetch succeeds.\n\n## Would have broken something if followed\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `README.md` | `yarn e2e` — no such script; it is `e2e:ci` |\n| `tests/README.md`, `MIGRATION-NOTES` | `instance_type: ubuntu-latest`\n— the live workflow omits it deliberately and explains why: the reusable\nworkflow calls `ec2metadata`, absent on GitHub-hosted runners |\n| `MIGRATION-NOTES` ×3 | points at `.chachalog/README.md`, which does\nnot exist; the guide is `.github/changelog-fragments.md` |\n\n## Wrong about behaviour\n\n- *\"No findings list is capped\"* — **five of six are** (500, 200, 200,\n100, 5000), and only the freshness list emits a truncation flag the UI\ncan show.\n- `RATE_MAX_CALLS` described as metering \"the expensive actions\" — it\nmeters **the crawler check only**. The scan hardcodes 30; the AI report\nuses its own key.\n- *\"Four findings\"* above a five-row sitemap table.\n- *\"Seventeen checks\"* in one backlog, against eighteen in four other\ndocs.\n\n## Stale\n\n`.agents/README.md` — the file agents read first — said `CACHE_SCHEMA`\n*\"is at 5\"*. It is **8**. An agent obeying that invariant writes the\nwrong next value and the stale cached report survives, which is the\nexact symptom the same paragraph says has \"cost time twice\". It also\ncalled `RobotsRules` the only unit-testable class (eight others now are)\nand counted four documentation files (there are ten).\n\n`tests/README.md` said the repository has no unit tests. It has **354**.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **354 unit tests**, **133/133 Cypress**\n- Build clean on JDK 17\n\n## Not in this PR, on purpose\n\nThe audit surfaced a **live code defect**, verified: `saveSchedule`\nwrites eight keys into the Quartz job data; `ScanLifecycle.restore()`\nputs back five, dropping `TIMEOUT_MS`, `MAX_BYTES` and `MAX_PAGES`.\nAfter any bundle restart a scheduled scan silently runs with a\n**10,000-page cap instead of 2,000** and ignores the configured fetch\ntimeout and body limit — and `restore()` is the code `architecture.md`\ncredits with surviving a redeploy. It needs new wiring and belongs in\nits own PR.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"bd9db2e778a6cf96689e0dac57e7a6c67fd58e2a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/bd9db2e778a6cf96689e0dac57e7a6c67fd58e2a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/e67a635d5c7bf12736d0a4561af7ce5b11f75e38","verification":{"payload":"tree bd9db2e778a6cf96689e0dac57e7a6c67fd58e2a\nparent 6acd0f516faedf998bf2d3923ea2e0f234096dd8\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789549712 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789549712 +0200\n\ntest: assert what the visibility check reports, and correct the docs it contradicts (#23)\n\nTwo things at once, because they turned out to be the same thing: the\nCypress suite now asserts what a check **reports**, and the\ndocumentation now says what the code **does**.\n\n## The e2e suite asserted almost no results\n\n127 assertions, and essentially all of them about refusals — who gets\n403, what gets 400, which paths are rejected. The happy path proved a\nscan *happened*: `has(''run'')`, `startedAt` not null.\n\n`guestVisibility` was called three times in the whole suite, **all\ninside `rate-limit.spec.ts`**, to spend the per-user budget. Nothing\nasserted what it returns. Inverting `isolatedRestriction` and\n`gatedBranch` — telling a customer their members area is a\nmisconfiguration, or their misconfiguration is a members area — would\nnot have failed anything.\n\n### What the fixture now builds\n\n```\nhome                 readable\n  geo-gated          ACL inheritance broken  →  isolatedRestriction  (findings)\n    geo-gated-child  inherits the closure    →  gatedBranch          (deliberate)\n```\n\nTwo pages, not one, because **the distinction is the check''s whole\noutput**. A closed page under an open one is the permission somebody\nforgot; under a closed one it is a members area working as intended.\nReporting the second as a defect is how a check gets ignored.\n\nThis cannot be a unit test. In Jahia a server administrator is a\n**role**, and a role arrives through ACL entries — exactly what breaking\ninheritance removes, so after the break every account but `root` and the\nsystem session is denied. And the check reads `live` through a real\nguest session precisely because a system session bypasses the ACLs it is\nmeasuring. Mocking that session mocks the thing under test.\n\nOne assertion guards the rest: `scanned > 0`. If the fixture silently\nfails to build, every other assertion would pass by describing an empty\nscan.\n\n**133/133**, up from 127.\n\n## The documentation audit found two CODE defects\n\nThe prose was right and the code was not — the shape this module keeps\nproducing.\n\n**`ci.startup.sh notests` never worked.** The header says *\"Pass\n`notests` to boot Jahia only\"*; the last line called the package''s\n`ci.startup` with no `\"$@\"`, so the flag was read off this script''s\narguments and never handed on. The documented \"boot Jahia only\" step\nbooted the stack **and ran every spec**. Forwarded in all four wrappers.\nI wrote that script in #12.\n\n**A comment misled the README.** `PublicUrls.derived()` said *\"the site\ndoes not say where it lives, so neither can we … `PUBLIC_BASE_URL` is\nthe way to say it\"* — then returned `http://localhost:8080` and fetched\nit. On a box serving several sites that is the **default** site, so the\ncheck reports another site''s pages and robots.txt as if they were yours,\nquietly, because the fetch succeeds.\n\n## Would have broken something if followed\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `README.md` | `yarn e2e` — no such script; it is `e2e:ci` |\n| `tests/README.md`, `MIGRATION-NOTES` | `instance_type: ubuntu-latest`\n— the live workflow omits it deliberately and explains why: the reusable\nworkflow calls `ec2metadata`, absent on GitHub-hosted runners |\n| `MIGRATION-NOTES` ×3 | points at `.chachalog/README.md`, which does\nnot exist; the guide is `.github/changelog-fragments.md` |\n\n## Wrong about behaviour\n\n- *\"No findings list is capped\"* — **five of six are** (500, 200, 200,\n100, 5000), and only the freshness list emits a truncation flag the UI\ncan show.\n- `RATE_MAX_CALLS` described as metering \"the expensive actions\" — it\nmeters **the crawler check only**. The scan hardcodes 30; the AI report\nuses its own key.\n- *\"Four findings\"* above a five-row sitemap table.\n- *\"Seventeen checks\"* in one backlog, against eighteen in four other\ndocs.\n\n## Stale\n\n`.agents/README.md` — the file agents read first — said `CACHE_SCHEMA`\n*\"is at 5\"*. It is **8**. An agent obeying that invariant writes the\nwrong next value and the stale cached report survives, which is the\nexact symptom the same paragraph says has \"cost time twice\". It also\ncalled `RobotsRules` the only unit-testable class (eight others now are)\nand counted four documentation files (there are ten).\n\n`tests/README.md` said the repository has no unit tests. It has **354**.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **354 unit tests**, **133/133 Cypress**\n- Build clean on JDK 17\n\n## Not in this PR, on purpose\n\nThe audit surfaced a **live code defect**, verified: `saveSchedule`\nwrites eight keys into the Quartz job data; `ScanLifecycle.restore()`\nputs back five, dropping `TIMEOUT_MS`, `MAX_BYTES` and `MAX_PAGES`.\nAfter any bundle restart a scheduled scan silently runs with a\n**10,000-page cap instead of 2,000** and ignores the configured fetch\ntimeout and body limit — and `restore()` is the code `architecture.md`\ncredits with surviving a redeploy. It needs new wiring and belongs in\nits own PR.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqlyQCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAFYIQACvRro0JsYTPeX+WA2RfpTun\n2QPc3cyEJNcv5Mq77TNNtAeyzs3DzY1oIlGfYZ0wgUVUxQDuLTod6d+MgOBs+4dD\n/Rk5NaWKqHVYSUD/UzwOKmqOQv5tf/b/uMKiTzDcalRJ5tVRdUcSO1uVrSUqQfOb\nRL0WhOBaHeWI6OqZO6m5Gtacz0mqyKqGXTTlMMiBQLbJkBhFLgU5xvuvqQUnY62F\nnmxHDEMR04uf6kyeok9H30ltU4VgOv/MW9DsZAECMayrjrmAzPHth0BN4gMxcPah\nNRU/1YQOp0C9QG2wV9AzXgYn+l5KjjWFYya8nYbqJjLI6XS6WdaKoBuY+wvuitqE\nG3/kbrXfXuo43BL43SLm3U+NC0Md2uGhV8dMf4PPlh0PkwP93oLhcWO0o1sb87Iv\nM0/zl7GM5QWbGLWishTC3VOMtiR4N2xY3pDfSiZ4IVk15qwkC1rz2z71ZSDfUYKb\nPTku0+cZ0jIjPVvT2uQJF6GFCK3Uk9FPHk87CYJdE5lDVtgeARiVEaxNi3nFh1vO\nV0kGrnE7nD1ks8INmgbZiTbN/OOzdSQ/uJSkrwaE8zBKSTnsPQti5zhNX99MVSf9\nOohgLnIjeMYZtWSet9u5VzW2t3Tv268jaA8EagI92BJe2fFbxJE0yRikUru0jbAP\n8ZimUchL709/0QFqIVqP\n=cyvG\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T09:08:33Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/e67a635d5c7bf12736d0a4561af7ce5b11f75e38","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKGU2N2E2MzVkNWM3YmYxMjczNmQwYTQ1NjFhZjdjZTViMTFmNzVlMzg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/6acd0f516faedf998bf2d3923ea2e0f234096dd8","sha":"6acd0f516faedf998bf2d3923ea2e0f234096dd8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/6acd0f516faedf998bf2d3923ea2e0f234096dd8"}],"sha":"e67a635d5c7bf12736d0a4561af7ce5b11f75e38","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/e67a635d5c7bf12736d0a4561af7ce5b11f75e38"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/6acd0f516faedf998bf2d3923ea2e0f234096dd8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T08:28:22Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T08:28:22Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: seven defects the characterisation tests pinned (#22)\n\nSeven defects the characterisation tests from #16 and #19 had pinned\nrather than hidden. Each was pinned as a passing test with a `SUSPECT:`\ncomment; each test now states the corrected behaviour with the old one\nnamed.\n\n## The one that matters most\n\n`jsonLdTypes` was read out of the `<script>` block with a **regex, never\nan actual parse**. So a block whose JSON is broken still reported its\ntypes, and `GeoScore`''s `structuredData` check **passed the page** —\nwhile a crawler that actually parses the block reads it as declaring\nnothing.\n\nThat is a false pass on the exact thing the check exists to find, and it\nis the worst direction for this module to be wrong in: it tells a\ncustomer their structured data is fine when the consumer it was written\nfor cannot read it.\n\nParsed now, following `@graph`, nesting, and `@type` as either a string\nor an array. Every shape the scraper handled still works — switching to\na parser failed **exactly one** of the 85 `PageFetch` tests, the\nmalformed-JSON case, which is the point.\n\n## RobotsEditor, which edits a file on the customer''s live site\n\nIts class comment says it leaves what it was not asked about *\"byte for\nbyte as it found it: the wildcard group, comments, blank lines, Sitemap\nlines\"*. It did not, in four ways:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| a no-op merge | **added** a trailing newline the file did not have |\n| a body of only blank lines | came back **empty** |\n| a CRLF file, edited once | came back **mixing CRLF and LF** |\n| a new token on an empty file | opened with **two blank lines** nobody\nwrote |\n\nFixed at the promise rather than at the symptoms: `applyOne` now reports\nwhether it changed anything, and a merge that changed nothing returns\nthe input **string**, not a re-render of it. That makes the whole class\nof no-op diffs impossible instead of fixing them one at a time — and it\nis what surfaced the fourth, which was not on the list.\n\n`render` also takes the file''s own line ending, and an empty body parses\nto no blocks at all.\n\n## LlmsFreshness\n\nTwo emptiness guards disagreed: `served` used `trim()`, `generated` did\nnot. A generated body of nothing but whitespace therefore counted as\ncontent, the link scan found nothing in it, and **every page `llms.txt`\nlists came back stale with `why=\"gone\"`** — the loudest answer the\nreport has, for what is really \"we could not generate anything\".\n\n`listedPaths` was also the one array with no cap. It relied on the\n*generator''s* link budget to stay small, which says nothing about the\n**served** file — written by whoever wrote it. A file listing ten\nthousand paths put all ten thousand into every report, every scan. The\ncount still reports the truth; only the listing is cut.\n\n## A depth cap, and why the comment on it is worth reading\n\nThe JSON-LD walk is depth-limited. I first assumed `org.json` bounded\nnesting itself, and wrote a test asserting a 2000-level document was\nrefused. It **was** refused — by a bug in my own test generator, which\nemitted `{\"@type\":\"Top\",\"n\":{,\"n\":{`. Measured properly, `org.json` in\nthis version parses 2000 levels without complaint and contributes no\nbound at all.\n\nSo the cap is the only thing stopping a page the module does not control\nfrom choosing how deep this recurses. Both bounds now have a test that\nsays which is which.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **354 unit tests** pass\n- **127/127 Cypress** assertions pass\n- Rebased onto `main` after #7''s groupId move; build clean on JDK 17\n\nSequenced after #13–#20.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"5bec15b079e3307cb25d189d3a4c18e565e4b81c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/5bec15b079e3307cb25d189d3a4c18e565e4b81c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/6acd0f516faedf998bf2d3923ea2e0f234096dd8","verification":{"payload":"tree 5bec15b079e3307cb25d189d3a4c18e565e4b81c\nparent cf56e8a7c2061c3e675181f9560891474f388179\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789547302 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789547302 +0200\n\nfix: seven defects the characterisation tests pinned (#22)\n\nSeven defects the characterisation tests from #16 and #19 had pinned\nrather than hidden. Each was pinned as a passing test with a `SUSPECT:`\ncomment; each test now states the corrected behaviour with the old one\nnamed.\n\n## The one that matters most\n\n`jsonLdTypes` was read out of the `<script>` block with a **regex, never\nan actual parse**. So a block whose JSON is broken still reported its\ntypes, and `GeoScore`''s `structuredData` check **passed the page** —\nwhile a crawler that actually parses the block reads it as declaring\nnothing.\n\nThat is a false pass on the exact thing the check exists to find, and it\nis the worst direction for this module to be wrong in: it tells a\ncustomer their structured data is fine when the consumer it was written\nfor cannot read it.\n\nParsed now, following `@graph`, nesting, and `@type` as either a string\nor an array. Every shape the scraper handled still works — switching to\na parser failed **exactly one** of the 85 `PageFetch` tests, the\nmalformed-JSON case, which is the point.\n\n## RobotsEditor, which edits a file on the customer''s live site\n\nIts class comment says it leaves what it was not asked about *\"byte for\nbyte as it found it: the wildcard group, comments, blank lines, Sitemap\nlines\"*. It did not, in four ways:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| a no-op merge | **added** a trailing newline the file did not have |\n| a body of only blank lines | came back **empty** |\n| a CRLF file, edited once | came back **mixing CRLF and LF** |\n| a new token on an empty file | opened with **two blank lines** nobody\nwrote |\n\nFixed at the promise rather than at the symptoms: `applyOne` now reports\nwhether it changed anything, and a merge that changed nothing returns\nthe input **string**, not a re-render of it. That makes the whole class\nof no-op diffs impossible instead of fixing them one at a time — and it\nis what surfaced the fourth, which was not on the list.\n\n`render` also takes the file''s own line ending, and an empty body parses\nto no blocks at all.\n\n## LlmsFreshness\n\nTwo emptiness guards disagreed: `served` used `trim()`, `generated` did\nnot. A generated body of nothing but whitespace therefore counted as\ncontent, the link scan found nothing in it, and **every page `llms.txt`\nlists came back stale with `why=\"gone\"`** — the loudest answer the\nreport has, for what is really \"we could not generate anything\".\n\n`listedPaths` was also the one array with no cap. It relied on the\n*generator''s* link budget to stay small, which says nothing about the\n**served** file — written by whoever wrote it. A file listing ten\nthousand paths put all ten thousand into every report, every scan. The\ncount still reports the truth; only the listing is cut.\n\n## A depth cap, and why the comment on it is worth reading\n\nThe JSON-LD walk is depth-limited. I first assumed `org.json` bounded\nnesting itself, and wrote a test asserting a 2000-level document was\nrefused. It **was** refused — by a bug in my own test generator, which\nemitted `{\"@type\":\"Top\",\"n\":{,\"n\":{`. Measured properly, `org.json` in\nthis version parses 2000 levels without complaint and contributes no\nbound at all.\n\nSo the cap is the only thing stopping a page the module does not control\nfrom choosing how deep this recurses. Both bounds now have a test that\nsays which is which.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **354 unit tests** pass\n- **127/127 Cypress** assertions pass\n- Rebased onto `main` after #7''s groupId move; build clean on JDK 17\n\nSequenced after #13–#20.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqlMmCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAuhkQADr/wULz4eCfoiVwkEs/B1yM\nywBk3KpwVH5anHqSWX+I7RhYkJC15QVJXdRuP5vqkaTCs4ToRPElbw58OExlqqts\nX/yN3fCwR8yTRFWQWDGbPvR/gyEbkSmCtr055zmnML6GBf9XY5qu3ydGbugPoU1r\nfpFm00iEFNT0fO6qpUylFwNgRGz1LEMabkLp8SFqGB2gUKxyTPdye4PEMb6UXIBJ\n/2Ae9MP67rMBHuYCeEMrVAwac5bt0EIU6QflDyoEthsrOaCrTmvD7NIBpkTISzSK\n+0Rrde6bEuqrs/wJkCOajYp8+jne1lRggL21aSoPb14o4VBZd33xI5J1eSxOA7DN\nETwrRLGbMhBHcTIxQVpdNU5DP4y8gEffrMRw9Kk6hnHzp5uhGRcSypgqJRv02XBw\nw6V5Rlfv8hGUrIRIGWEtP0hYvbw1WHyaM6bTKEMg4yzKyvIrOUObqeejEUuwSFwb\n+P9/NqT4yXJquOXpAJPE6tmArwYHA+orsmsAx7DOzYTAmVdoeqNm0JVp5azbgL9w\n0qwkRlGKZVbSZVFaz2ujuZljg8uks2mrmpVHgDpQgPem+yCMAmG2leKRUcVUb73w\nGZu+ZCLbVdJJ5KdljKCISlcK+8pnzKjXPNZFeaKsFnlH8dZXskVoz16qKS5wd0xO\nSvC/7x/ycwUQzLwQiblh\n=pTHs\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T08:28:22Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/6acd0f516faedf998bf2d3923ea2e0f234096dd8","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDZhY2QwZjUxNmZhZWRmOTk4YmYyZDM5MjNlYTJlMGYyMzQwOTZkZDg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/cf56e8a7c2061c3e675181f9560891474f388179","sha":"cf56e8a7c2061c3e675181f9560891474f388179","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/cf56e8a7c2061c3e675181f9560891474f388179"}],"sha":"6acd0f516faedf998bf2d3923ea2e0f234096dd8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/6acd0f516faedf998bf2d3923ea2e0f234096dd8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/722535?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/smonier","id":722535,"login":"smonier","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcyMjUzNQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/cf56e8a7c2061c3e675181f9560891474f388179/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T08:03:30Z","email":"smonier@Jahia.com","name":"Stephane Monier"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T08:03:30Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: drop the imports the decomposition left behind (#21)\n\n`main`''s quality gate is red on **10 New Maintainability Issues** and\n**62 minutes of added technical debt**. The security and reliability\nratings are already clear, so this is what is left of the security\nteam''s \"the gate has to pass\".\n\nNine of the ten are in `SitemapCheck`, which #18 and #19 emptied of its\nJCR query code without taking the imports with it — `PublicUrls`,\n`JCRCallback`, `JCRNodeWrapper`, `JCRSessionWrapper`, `JCRTemplate`,\n`NodeIterator`, `Query`, `Calendar`, `Locale`. The tenth is a\n`LinkedHashMap` in `SiteFilesChecker`. Not one of them is referenced\nanywhere in its own file.\n\n## Why none of the merged PRs was at fault\n\nEvery one of #13 through #20 passed its own gate. A pull-request gate\nmeasures that pull request''s diff; `main`''s new-code period covers\neverything since 1.3.0, which is a much wider net — an issue on an\nunchanged line of a changed file counts there and does not count in a\nPR.\n\n## How they were found\n\nBranch analyses publish no check-run annotations and\n`sonarqube.jahia.com` refuses an unauthenticated API read, so the count\nwas visible and the list was not. Scanning the Java changed since the\n`1_3_0` tag for the mechanical rules turned up exactly ten unused\nimports — and ten issues at the remediation cost `java:S1128` carries\nalso accounts for the 62 minutes, which is what makes the match more\nthan a coincidence.\n\nEach removal was checked against its own file for any use of the\nidentifier outside the import block before being taken out, and the\nmodule builds.\n\n**This PR''s own gate cannot prove the fix.** Removing imports introduces\nno new code, so it passes either way; only the next analysis of `main`\nwill show the gate green.","tree":{"sha":"f9d38641041714ba746f5748a22b3b08881d273b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/f9d38641041714ba746f5748a22b3b08881d273b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/cf56e8a7c2061c3e675181f9560891474f388179","verification":{"payload":"tree f9d38641041714ba746f5748a22b3b08881d273b\nparent c1eb35b2b10249476062f091a93f7349e02dda25\nauthor Stephane Monier <smonier@Jahia.com> 1789545810 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789545810 +0200\n\nfix: drop the imports the decomposition left behind (#21)\n\n`main`''s quality gate is red on **10 New Maintainability Issues** and\n**62 minutes of added technical debt**. The security and reliability\nratings are already clear, so this is what is left of the security\nteam''s \"the gate has to pass\".\n\nNine of the ten are in `SitemapCheck`, which #18 and #19 emptied of its\nJCR query code without taking the imports with it — `PublicUrls`,\n`JCRCallback`, `JCRNodeWrapper`, `JCRSessionWrapper`, `JCRTemplate`,\n`NodeIterator`, `Query`, `Calendar`, `Locale`. The tenth is a\n`LinkedHashMap` in `SiteFilesChecker`. Not one of them is referenced\nanywhere in its own file.\n\n## Why none of the merged PRs was at fault\n\nEvery one of #13 through #20 passed its own gate. A pull-request gate\nmeasures that pull request''s diff; `main`''s new-code period covers\neverything since 1.3.0, which is a much wider net — an issue on an\nunchanged line of a changed file counts there and does not count in a\nPR.\n\n## How they were found\n\nBranch analyses publish no check-run annotations and\n`sonarqube.jahia.com` refuses an unauthenticated API read, so the count\nwas visible and the list was not. Scanning the Java changed since the\n`1_3_0` tag for the mechanical rules turned up exactly ten unused\nimports — and ten issues at the remediation cost `java:S1128` carries\nalso accounts for the 62 minutes, which is what makes the match more\nthan a coincidence.\n\nEach removal was checked against its own file for any use of the\nidentifier outside the import block before being taken out, and the\nmodule builds.\n\n**This PR''s own gate cannot prove the fix.** Removing imports introduces\nno new code, so it passes either way; only the next analysis of `main`\nwill show the gate green.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqk1SCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAUlAQADttyPcCg2eZo8jvR3CRC27n\nix5mxUhEUSkiSyUnPEfVZ3JxWCfEb1QULcf6tm4ZKxVaOnjkkZTnLTMUWw+1jlVE\n55gjwAagQCLChyDFAfYUZeCTSRu/+7CepXAy3tmfpBNz7XMlkw9AuIImPLwBb7tQ\n2zx/eXxkvw0FisAKX6PM/CovfDKOMb67S0RwwrTHGk17yCXo+MzE51Jb+q77W31+\n/KtOhrv3ahj6PM/7HTY38WWeOQw8HyZcwPaYGE9Opievc9JXgHZwucGr7lzOfNsA\nT1b+hWVC1ZLrgW5gljJ8uJ7fPsHanwQHQRyOjLDalpKAx4VNHNYbKJIz4Ap9dgr7\n9YIN8w+92cZHyubsf8zDIvMbm3XRKTWILoRAal8ku1SindepfpHDObiG+p2TYYRj\nAr/u0vLSwjxH51bRCJugsBlcjQA77nLOWTvq8e0pzLL/Lx0ctEejeM9DcZO6HnwV\ncAl9CsMveEhKqwexOwb8q2TsIRsosupQ+UEC8xboql136UvY95cs8NqTKFeP866H\noSuUeLypu1RLz9Qg/vWBtRaoDVhoIKST6QW+iX+v33gbKggDM3u8bGWoMWf6+v2J\n6CPyGsdSNul0sIk5ZHiOTHk89EB83he6AojqkE5rtebQ+jH/Q99+2/Du/dvp6BTA\noG30lrF28XkK0Q9VO0hh\n=a7Pq\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T08:03:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/cf56e8a7c2061c3e675181f9560891474f388179","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKGNmNTZlOGE3YzIwNjFjM2U2NzUxODFmOTU2MDg5MTQ3NGYzODgxNzk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/c1eb35b2b10249476062f091a93f7349e02dda25","sha":"c1eb35b2b10249476062f091a93f7349e02dda25","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/c1eb35b2b10249476062f091a93f7349e02dda25"}],"sha":"cf56e8a7c2061c3e675181f9560891474f388179","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/cf56e8a7c2061c3e675181f9560891474f388179"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/722535?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/smonier","id":722535,"login":"smonier","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcyMjUzNQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/c1eb35b2b10249476062f091a93f7349e02dda25/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T07:38:59Z","email":"smonier@Jahia.com","name":"Stephane Monier"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T07:38:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"chore: move to the org.jahia.community groupId (#7)\n\nStacked on #6, so its two commits show here too. Once #6 merges GitHub\nretargets this to `main`.\n\nThe module was published under `org.jahia.se.modules`, the Solution\nEngineering namespace. It is a community module now, so it moves to\n`org.jahia.community` — where the 49 other community artifacts on\n`devtools.jahia.com` already sit, and where\n`graphql-extension-provisioning`, the repository this one''s settings\nwere copied from, is released through this same shared CI.\n\nFour files. Only the coordinates move.\n\n## What deliberately does not move\n\n**The Java package.** It names the OSGi configuration PID, so renaming\nit would leave every deployed `org.jahia.se.modules.georeadiness.cfg`\norphaned and silently unread — and the setting that would go missing is\n`AI_API_KEY`, whose absence reads as a broken Report tab rather than an\nunconfigured one. `content-reports-react` ships the same way: community\ngroupId over an `org.jahia.modules` package.\n\nIt is also what the quality gate cares about. A first attempt moved the\npackage too, and Sonar counted all 44 renamed files as new: 258\nfindings, every one of them pre-existing code the rename had made look\nnew, none in a file the branch otherwise touched. Renaming files is not\na diff the new-code gate can see through.\n\n**The Sonar project key**, pinned in the pom. Sonar keys a project by\n`groupId:artifactId`, so the move alone would start an empty project\nwith no reference branch — again, the whole module as new code. The key\nis an identifier, not an address; it stays where the analyses are. An\nadministrator can rename the project key in SonarQube, after which the\nproperty is deleted.\n\n## What is unchanged\n\n- `Bundle-SymbolicName: geo-readiness` — Jahia upgrades the module in\nplace, and the module id sites are enabled against does not move.\n- `Jahia-GroupId` is `org.jahia.community`, still not\n`org.jahia.modules`, so the runtime check that uninstalls unsigned\nbundles still does not apply.\n- The OSGi configuration file, the Java package, the front-end bundle\nand the bytecode target.\n\n## Verified on luxe\n\nBuilt, the old bundle uninstalled, this one installed and `ACTIVE`. `GET\n/modules/geo-readiness/report` answers\n`{\"provider\":\"deepseek\",\"model\":\"deepseek-v4-flash\",\"enabled\":true}`\nfrom the untouched config file, and `site-scan` still returns the stored\nscan, so the reinstall cost nothing.","tree":{"sha":"15b539873c23a151d2b051210a9b4056bfc2ac8c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/15b539873c23a151d2b051210a9b4056bfc2ac8c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/c1eb35b2b10249476062f091a93f7349e02dda25","verification":{"payload":"tree 15b539873c23a151d2b051210a9b4056bfc2ac8c\nparent 0a91b4108526f6f54f163862a1b89803f796fd5b\nauthor Stephane Monier <smonier@Jahia.com> 1789544339 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789544339 +0200\n\nchore: move to the org.jahia.community groupId (#7)\n\nStacked on #6, so its two commits show here too. Once #6 merges GitHub\nretargets this to `main`.\n\nThe module was published under `org.jahia.se.modules`, the Solution\nEngineering namespace. It is a community module now, so it moves to\n`org.jahia.community` — where the 49 other community artifacts on\n`devtools.jahia.com` already sit, and where\n`graphql-extension-provisioning`, the repository this one''s settings\nwere copied from, is released through this same shared CI.\n\nFour files. Only the coordinates move.\n\n## What deliberately does not move\n\n**The Java package.** It names the OSGi configuration PID, so renaming\nit would leave every deployed `org.jahia.se.modules.georeadiness.cfg`\norphaned and silently unread — and the setting that would go missing is\n`AI_API_KEY`, whose absence reads as a broken Report tab rather than an\nunconfigured one. `content-reports-react` ships the same way: community\ngroupId over an `org.jahia.modules` package.\n\nIt is also what the quality gate cares about. A first attempt moved the\npackage too, and Sonar counted all 44 renamed files as new: 258\nfindings, every one of them pre-existing code the rename had made look\nnew, none in a file the branch otherwise touched. Renaming files is not\na diff the new-code gate can see through.\n\n**The Sonar project key**, pinned in the pom. Sonar keys a project by\n`groupId:artifactId`, so the move alone would start an empty project\nwith no reference branch — again, the whole module as new code. The key\nis an identifier, not an address; it stays where the analyses are. An\nadministrator can rename the project key in SonarQube, after which the\nproperty is deleted.\n\n## What is unchanged\n\n- `Bundle-SymbolicName: geo-readiness` — Jahia upgrades the module in\nplace, and the module id sites are enabled against does not move.\n- `Jahia-GroupId` is `org.jahia.community`, still not\n`org.jahia.modules`, so the runtime check that uninstalls unsigned\nbundles still does not apply.\n- The OSGi configuration file, the Java package, the front-end bundle\nand the bytecode target.\n\n## Verified on luxe\n\nBuilt, the old bundle uninstalled, this one installed and `ACTIVE`. `GET\n/modules/geo-readiness/report` answers\n`{\"provider\":\"deepseek\",\"model\":\"deepseek-v4-flash\",\"enabled\":true}`\nfrom the untouched config file, and `site-scan` still returns the stored\nscan, so the reinstall cost nothing.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqkeTCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAADUQAH7YsZqvtNLLa5SdX0upteQE\nZOdEan4oItF6ATnsWsnlVlasmW+duPDWqFTldlIwTieWSNwc50RdFBfJ5+MUO6cV\nBHrunNR+q6jHw1jigvVmeQ8Z3hfMnH6YIMa1cEDXQMID7A49w9bJwzhBp7e2ud34\nPXTGEtEvEUUoThhhrpFTzmJtdI16HgnzgpskD+pKWZ0nx8fo0Glt55Nu4IGMkafh\n1Vcf8WyAnyBDf/iBt+cbjcu3r4HQm2fU2L2wdEDADpFxO3oSgH7Cr/Tzt+2xc9oQ\n2kTNgabATtNbD//xXWCKuLP7U0nw+UoVjriohFbSzq3whSzHwS2q1Dk3IX+gzMCT\n0jJdpDzlSQ+BCSAP00J1Dnsd4AYgV6Yg574nbERuD/W6DqB5h9vjcvQdkTRmmJ/L\nhrAbnrEegT3adL72bWUsz6K+199fc/YGDuXSo9SBYQ3TQkgLQ4TedMqcd0zXPH0r\nPKG1SNDtxPe3pnETlME2RUv+muqGsniN87nqPRpWjt7TSCP8p8sOi3W0GAyyh1M9\n5nrMplm7PjtDiXPmJNGjZ2oTcsQfiMwzOMmw+lK4FF7FBAyfyLEh1p7mbg2x9vOh\nyLGzfW/kvtSFmFkeYzyaUgUn7pj6/md7vv3YqfwsGrIwis8VDNUiPKeKIBYIA67a\n4cE9qRRonbkBAs61a7y2\n=T9OR\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T07:38:59Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/c1eb35b2b10249476062f091a93f7349e02dda25","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKGMxZWIzNWIyYjEwMjQ5NDc2MDYyZjA5MWE5M2Y3MzQ5ZTAyZGRhMjU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/0a91b4108526f6f54f163862a1b89803f796fd5b","sha":"0a91b4108526f6f54f163862a1b89803f796fd5b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/0a91b4108526f6f54f163862a1b89803f796fd5b"}],"sha":"c1eb35b2b10249476062f091a93f7349e02dda25","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/c1eb35b2b10249476062f091a93f7349e02dda25"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/0a91b4108526f6f54f163862a1b89803f796fd5b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T07:18:42Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T07:18:42Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"perf: read fetched documents in proportion to their size (#20)\n\nSupersedes #10, which this replaces rather than updates: the rebase\nneeded a force-push over that branch and it was cleaner to keep the\noriginal intact. **Every commit here keeps @smonier''s authorship.** What\nthe rebase changed is recorded at the bottom — two commits dropped, and\nthree behaviour differences the original verification could not have\nseen.\nCloses the 23 open `java/polynomial-redos` alerts.\n\nEvery check in this module reads markup written by somebody else.\nFinding a tag with a pattern — `<img\\s[^>]*>` and its kind — is linear\non ordinary markup and far worse on some documents, because the engine\ncan re-run the same scan from every position a tag could have started\nat. One document took **11 minutes 30 seconds** of server time to read.\nIt now takes **36 milliseconds**.\n\n## Why not simply bound the patterns\n\nThat trades one fault for another, and it was measured rather than\nassumed. A 400-character limit on a tag stopped counting five of the\ntwelve images on the demo home page: tags there reach 681 characters,\nand a single `srcset` or data URL goes further still. Any bound low\nenough to be safe is also low enough to quietly change what the module\nreports.\n\n## What changed\n\nTags are no longer found by pattern. `Markup` walks a document once and\nhands each tag on as a short string of its own; patterns are left to\nread one attribute out of one tag, where a bound costs nothing and\ncannot hide anything. The same walk replaces the sitemap''s `<url>` and\n`<sitemap>` blocks and the element bodies behind the word count. What\nremains either runs against a single tag, or asks for what it wants in a\nway that has only one reading — `<loc>([^<]{0,2000})</loc>` rather than\n`<loc>\\s*(.*?)\\s*</loc>`, trimmed afterwards.\n\nAlso here: the four workflows this repository owns now declare a\nread-only token. The repository default is write, which every action —\nand every dependency of an action — inherited on every pull request.\nNothing in them needs it: the release pushes over SSH, Nexus and\nDependency-Track get their own credentials.\n\n## Verified equal, not assumed\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Crawler check, 3 pages × 16 agents | byte-identical |\n| Full site scan, 191 pages | byte-identical |\n\nThe first attempt at this change did regress: the image count fell from\n12 to 7 on the home page, and the comparison is what caught it.\n\n---\n\n# What the rebase changed\n\n## Two commits dropped, superseded by #14\n\n`fix: say why a back-office request failed, and meet contrast on the\nmarks` and `fix: make the charts readable, and stop tabbing through\nevery bar` both landed in #14 while this PR was open — and #14 reached\nthe same conclusions independently: remove the per-mark tab stops, put\nthe severity word inside the matrix cell, drop `role=\"img\"` from the\nstacked bar. Re-applying them would have been a no-op at best and a\nconflict at worst.\n\nEverything else is kept, with the original authorship on every commit.\n\n## Three behaviour differences this PR did not claim\n\nThe module now has 352 unit tests, including 85 that pin every field\n`PageFetch.analyse` emits. Cherry-picking the `Markup` rewrite failed\nexactly three of them, all in the same direction:\n\n| Input | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| `<img>` | not counted at all | counted, and as lacking alt |\n| `<img/>` | not counted at all | counted, and as lacking alt |\n| `<h2/>` | not counted | counted |\n\nThe old patterns required whitespace or `>` immediately after the tag\nname, so a tag with no attributes matched nothing. An attributeless\nimage was excluded from **both** `images` and `imagesWithAlt`, rather\nthan counted as one missing its alt text — which is the accessibility\nfinding that check exists to make. `Markup` tokenises the tag instead of\nmatching around it, so the name ends where it ends.\n\n**These are fixes, and they mean the \"byte-identical\" claim above is not\nquite right.** That is no criticism of the verification, which was the\nbest available when the module had no unit tests: a whole-output diff\ncan only catch what the sample content happens to contain, and 191 pages\nof demo content evidently had no attributeless tags. The three tests\nthat pinned the old behaviour now state the new one, with the old named\nin a comment.\n\n## Verification after the rebase\n\n- **352 unit tests** pass\n- **127/127 Cypress** assertions pass\n- Build clean on JDK 17\n\nSequenced after #13–#19, all merged.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\nCo-authored-by: Stephane Monier <smonier@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"676d3fff77878560927e38770151739e198670ca","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/676d3fff77878560927e38770151739e198670ca"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/0a91b4108526f6f54f163862a1b89803f796fd5b","verification":{"payload":"tree 676d3fff77878560927e38770151739e198670ca\nparent 36d894f80fe23ce7ec291107d56e21c17d34a2b8\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789543122 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789543122 +0200\n\nperf: read fetched documents in proportion to their size (#20)\n\nSupersedes #10, which this replaces rather than updates: the rebase\nneeded a force-push over that branch and it was cleaner to keep the\noriginal intact. **Every commit here keeps @smonier''s authorship.** What\nthe rebase changed is recorded at the bottom — two commits dropped, and\nthree behaviour differences the original verification could not have\nseen.\nCloses the 23 open `java/polynomial-redos` alerts.\n\nEvery check in this module reads markup written by somebody else.\nFinding a tag with a pattern — `<img\\s[^>]*>` and its kind — is linear\non ordinary markup and far worse on some documents, because the engine\ncan re-run the same scan from every position a tag could have started\nat. One document took **11 minutes 30 seconds** of server time to read.\nIt now takes **36 milliseconds**.\n\n## Why not simply bound the patterns\n\nThat trades one fault for another, and it was measured rather than\nassumed. A 400-character limit on a tag stopped counting five of the\ntwelve images on the demo home page: tags there reach 681 characters,\nand a single `srcset` or data URL goes further still. Any bound low\nenough to be safe is also low enough to quietly change what the module\nreports.\n\n## What changed\n\nTags are no longer found by pattern. `Markup` walks a document once and\nhands each tag on as a short string of its own; patterns are left to\nread one attribute out of one tag, where a bound costs nothing and\ncannot hide anything. The same walk replaces the sitemap''s `<url>` and\n`<sitemap>` blocks and the element bodies behind the word count. What\nremains either runs against a single tag, or asks for what it wants in a\nway that has only one reading — `<loc>([^<]{0,2000})</loc>` rather than\n`<loc>\\s*(.*?)\\s*</loc>`, trimmed afterwards.\n\nAlso here: the four workflows this repository owns now declare a\nread-only token. The repository default is write, which every action —\nand every dependency of an action — inherited on every pull request.\nNothing in them needs it: the release pushes over SSH, Nexus and\nDependency-Track get their own credentials.\n\n## Verified equal, not assumed\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Crawler check, 3 pages × 16 agents | byte-identical |\n| Full site scan, 191 pages | byte-identical |\n\nThe first attempt at this change did regress: the image count fell from\n12 to 7 on the home page, and the comparison is what caught it.\n\n---\n\n# What the rebase changed\n\n## Two commits dropped, superseded by #14\n\n`fix: say why a back-office request failed, and meet contrast on the\nmarks` and `fix: make the charts readable, and stop tabbing through\nevery bar` both landed in #14 while this PR was open — and #14 reached\nthe same conclusions independently: remove the per-mark tab stops, put\nthe severity word inside the matrix cell, drop `role=\"img\"` from the\nstacked bar. Re-applying them would have been a no-op at best and a\nconflict at worst.\n\nEverything else is kept, with the original authorship on every commit.\n\n## Three behaviour differences this PR did not claim\n\nThe module now has 352 unit tests, including 85 that pin every field\n`PageFetch.analyse` emits. Cherry-picking the `Markup` rewrite failed\nexactly three of them, all in the same direction:\n\n| Input | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| `<img>` | not counted at all | counted, and as lacking alt |\n| `<img/>` | not counted at all | counted, and as lacking alt |\n| `<h2/>` | not counted | counted |\n\nThe old patterns required whitespace or `>` immediately after the tag\nname, so a tag with no attributes matched nothing. An attributeless\nimage was excluded from **both** `images` and `imagesWithAlt`, rather\nthan counted as one missing its alt text — which is the accessibility\nfinding that check exists to make. `Markup` tokenises the tag instead of\nmatching around it, so the name ends where it ends.\n\n**These are fixes, and they mean the \"byte-identical\" claim above is not\nquite right.** That is no criticism of the verification, which was the\nbest available when the module had no unit tests: a whole-output diff\ncan only catch what the sample content happens to contain, and 191 pages\nof demo content evidently had no attributeless tags. The three tests\nthat pinned the old behaviour now state the new one, with the old named\nin a comment.\n\n## Verification after the rebase\n\n- **352 unit tests** pass\n- **127/127 Cypress** assertions pass\n- Build clean on JDK 17\n\nSequenced after #13–#19, all merged.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\nCo-authored-by: Stephane Monier <smonier@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqkLSCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAxt4QAAznKtijifYNSvpuzz5xxWmD\nhIJR6nEqAqYGEKEkt6brbjepg8ewYqi03gFs1IwbaNf5+7AO8eLXi4rVEFDA1cix\nHtbvYRSXzo3TX6RIqD1726FL5B7qkdhVEMOobRtjyPOkD44m+e/VTAbAhhema1oy\nfESSOzu771B055ClLbZ442TITarIARas0tF3ejJJICJYCXQwSSaQPu2VurbuxPJi\n5frvELAGGC4FkKQyq7N8hIegnbUjrhkOin6aEXFfuRF2c7PU6wsrG/XKKhV7RmLB\nLPmxc5luBlFjjR8xuvK7mFMxni98OuOtu9rgsXf0hFHvEl8E/HumToEuseVRt0Ch\nz91Du+6ty5G98bZHhO5KBFoHmM8Nou6ZzFXcY7n06Y0etOmTQfx+AvwT3pIDwDXm\nFW8MU3P8uxgY6nfKTylWQhiay+7WwbdB7j2B9N8+nrFkPLrTd9/Ek8BHwotfCQl4\nXOtloZvoROyRHlV4TJg36ZikHYOVSYYWCW5qDxWDmiqfYFsdU2vMDB+Gd6YYPAjZ\nnUK0YEM7H51QQNcwERa469DHZ6TDLxFfAQh++Wx8VkL+nNLunFsDSrJjPghOzKGC\nmz3axEfCfWwapUJfVrF3b/mSyLIjYIhJ8pq6ZjTSdQ3/hQnUJIjsmNK4X6bS54I2\n7fd10LS2SXu/VbqJHuwJ\n=6aMx\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T07:18:43Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/0a91b4108526f6f54f163862a1b89803f796fd5b","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDBhOTFiNDEwODUyNmY2ZjU0ZjE2Mzg2MmExYjg5ODAzZjc5NmZkNWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/36d894f80fe23ce7ec291107d56e21c17d34a2b8","sha":"36d894f80fe23ce7ec291107d56e21c17d34a2b8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/36d894f80fe23ce7ec291107d56e21c17d34a2b8"}],"sha":"0a91b4108526f6f54f163862a1b89803f796fd5b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/0a91b4108526f6f54f163862a1b89803f796fd5b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/36d894f80fe23ce7ec291107d56e21c17d34a2b8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T06:34:21Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T06:34:21Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test: characterisation tests and slice two of the decomposition (#19)\n\nMockito is added, and the useful finding is **how little of it is\nneeded**.\n\n## What Mockito actually costs here\n\n| Surface | Approach | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PageFetch.analyse`, `LinkGraph.report`, `RobotsEditor.parse` | **no\nmocking** — they take a String and a Map | free |\n| `GuestVisibility` decision helpers | one node mock | cheap, high\nsignal |\n| `scanSite`''s session chain | **skipped on purpose** | ~100 lines of\nwiring to verify that my own stubs return what I told them |\n\nThree of the eight remaining over-limit methods were never JCR-coupled\nat all. Every logic-bearing helper in `GuestVisibility` — `classify`,\n`row`, `parentOf`, `isNoindex`, `hasConditions`, `expiredEnd` — takes a\n**`JCRNodeWrapper`**, not a session. So the decisions are cheaply\ntestable, and what is left uncovered is thin plumbing. The class comment\nsays so rather than leaving a coverage gap unexplained.\n\n## Tests first, then the decomposition\n\n| Method | Complexity | Tests written first |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PageFetch.analyse` | 26 → 6 methods | 85 — every field and its **JSON\ntype** pinned |\n| `LinkGraph.report` | 28 → a `Findings` object + 4 | 32 — including the\nnull-vs-String `why` key |\n| `RobotsEditor.parse` | 16 → 3 methods | 28 — round-tripping a\ncustomer''s file |\n| `GuestVisibility` classifier | 21 | 14 |\n\n**352 unit tests**, up from 193. Coverage **8.2% → 16.7%**, nine classes\nat 98–100%. **127/127 Cypress.**\n\n## What the previously-green suite was hiding\n\n> `guestVisibility` is called three times across the Cypress specs —\n**all three inside `rate-limit.spec.ts`**, to spend the rate budget.\nThey assert only that it returned 200.\n\nNothing anywhere asserted what that scan *reports*. Inverting `isolated`\nand `gatedBranch` — telling a customer their members area is a\nmisconfiguration, or the reverse — would not have failed anything.\n\n## Eleven SUSPECT findings, pinned rather than fixed\n\nSo the refactor stays judged on behaviour preservation alone. Two are\nworth naming:\n\n1. **`jsonLdTypes` is regex-scraped, never parsed.** A page whose\nJSON-LD is syntactically invalid still yields its `@type` values, so\n`GeoScore.structuredData` passes it — while a crawler that actually\nparses the block sees none. A false pass on the exact thing the module\nexists to check.\n2. **`RobotsEditor` promises \"byte for byte\"** in its own class comment\n— *\"leaves everything else byte for byte as it found it: the wildcard\ngroup, comments, blank lines, Sitemap lines\"* — then strips trailing\nblank lines and emits **mixed line endings** into a CRLF file. It is\nediting a file on the customer''s live site.\n\nBoth are behaviour changes and belong in their own PR after this lands,\nas the robots `$` anchor did in #17.\n\n## Still over the limit, and why\n\n`PublishedMap.forSite` 18 · `ScanStore.schedules` 17 · `Languages.check`\n22 · `Languages.countBy` 22\n\nThese four are genuine session chains with no node-level seam to test\nthrough. They need either a different decomposition first (extract a\npure core, then test it) or an embedded repository. Not guessed at here.\n\nSequenced after #13–#18.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"060333b45a0968de8b9ce681dba813ca827dfb03","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/060333b45a0968de8b9ce681dba813ca827dfb03"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/36d894f80fe23ce7ec291107d56e21c17d34a2b8","verification":{"payload":"tree 060333b45a0968de8b9ce681dba813ca827dfb03\nparent 02be587bc777b2da1c5d1fb792cb34b5273e4253\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789540461 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789540461 +0200\n\ntest: characterisation tests and slice two of the decomposition (#19)\n\nMockito is added, and the useful finding is **how little of it is\nneeded**.\n\n## What Mockito actually costs here\n\n| Surface | Approach | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PageFetch.analyse`, `LinkGraph.report`, `RobotsEditor.parse` | **no\nmocking** — they take a String and a Map | free |\n| `GuestVisibility` decision helpers | one node mock | cheap, high\nsignal |\n| `scanSite`''s session chain | **skipped on purpose** | ~100 lines of\nwiring to verify that my own stubs return what I told them |\n\nThree of the eight remaining over-limit methods were never JCR-coupled\nat all. Every logic-bearing helper in `GuestVisibility` — `classify`,\n`row`, `parentOf`, `isNoindex`, `hasConditions`, `expiredEnd` — takes a\n**`JCRNodeWrapper`**, not a session. So the decisions are cheaply\ntestable, and what is left uncovered is thin plumbing. The class comment\nsays so rather than leaving a coverage gap unexplained.\n\n## Tests first, then the decomposition\n\n| Method | Complexity | Tests written first |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PageFetch.analyse` | 26 → 6 methods | 85 — every field and its **JSON\ntype** pinned |\n| `LinkGraph.report` | 28 → a `Findings` object + 4 | 32 — including the\nnull-vs-String `why` key |\n| `RobotsEditor.parse` | 16 → 3 methods | 28 — round-tripping a\ncustomer''s file |\n| `GuestVisibility` classifier | 21 | 14 |\n\n**352 unit tests**, up from 193. Coverage **8.2% → 16.7%**, nine classes\nat 98–100%. **127/127 Cypress.**\n\n## What the previously-green suite was hiding\n\n> `guestVisibility` is called three times across the Cypress specs —\n**all three inside `rate-limit.spec.ts`**, to spend the rate budget.\nThey assert only that it returned 200.\n\nNothing anywhere asserted what that scan *reports*. Inverting `isolated`\nand `gatedBranch` — telling a customer their members area is a\nmisconfiguration, or the reverse — would not have failed anything.\n\n## Eleven SUSPECT findings, pinned rather than fixed\n\nSo the refactor stays judged on behaviour preservation alone. Two are\nworth naming:\n\n1. **`jsonLdTypes` is regex-scraped, never parsed.** A page whose\nJSON-LD is syntactically invalid still yields its `@type` values, so\n`GeoScore.structuredData` passes it — while a crawler that actually\nparses the block sees none. A false pass on the exact thing the module\nexists to check.\n2. **`RobotsEditor` promises \"byte for byte\"** in its own class comment\n— *\"leaves everything else byte for byte as it found it: the wildcard\ngroup, comments, blank lines, Sitemap lines\"* — then strips trailing\nblank lines and emits **mixed line endings** into a CRLF file. It is\nediting a file on the customer''s live site.\n\nBoth are behaviour changes and belong in their own PR after this lands,\nas the robots `$` anchor did in #17.\n\n## Still over the limit, and why\n\n`PublishedMap.forSite` 18 · `ScanStore.schedules` 17 · `Languages.check`\n22 · `Languages.countBy` 22\n\nThese four are genuine session chains with no node-level seam to test\nthrough. They need either a different decomposition first (extract a\npure core, then test it) or an embedded repository. Not guessed at here.\n\nSequenced after #13–#18.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqjhtCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAqaEQAEx5c73bPETq+FZAYWqJ1h5A\neLpWZi751KSBvrGtExwjIUHPAgVnEhZdh5ee4tl/bIwx63u8iY6A3CxAlmDkgY5h\nO3M1BthjbDDSAiILNIWWVWqB5xARLKE/AzXy2aRv6jtE4L8/xaH2B7iStxeRzcB3\nV6JlO/Hecl1htSMLsBITqFI3voVs3YlQiWCXUzHZr2VuM+iTi/xDhpcs32EbMUwr\nuI8z8hSjvsqeEpjJGXwJpnaw9M/geQO2v2Fr45IFxZZZzvKJCyRLvOmdjj6RVjJ/\nLWRtHJiFkBf52/bmagLZt5WfemJ9K1t2p6sqgV4AnAvxMKTjLgXtZRgkGekVx+Ze\nSmMChhY0jXwRzTqldZl2To7rtiys/34X92MVYLt8q2lxkfRqkJLoKfkCSsY3BlEm\n0FU2DtLt09RUGrHZ8CbhUmYsSdAUei5Tb3FbAwt8BgT/18iIXqQWrkWZR8kQ42AQ\n52HbrR5TppjKjtnGmHLo8VGb4Sk2CQKTm06ac3ZbkqA08yofzWBw9vCbC2c9Z2me\n+xb3b9LRWs658VlWapA3MzQQanC29R2TpIqZZseHD9mVowr6jfJp/rv+I2DVNnzJ\nQdCNt45aY3mIeX9mkHj9GkFwIpMSMEyZ0J/KGBKBg7WDks0nsMlTlanwyuNAwgWA\n2yn+5JN5a7oBzhxxEqlb\n=xWpf\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T06:34:22Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/36d894f80fe23ce7ec291107d56e21c17d34a2b8","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDM2ZDg5NGY4MGZlMjNjZTdlYzI5MTEwN2Q1NmUyMWMxN2QzNGEyYjg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/02be587bc777b2da1c5d1fb792cb34b5273e4253","sha":"02be587bc777b2da1c5d1fb792cb34b5273e4253","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/02be587bc777b2da1c5d1fb792cb34b5273e4253"}],"sha":"36d894f80fe23ce7ec291107d56e21c17d34a2b8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/36d894f80fe23ce7ec291107d56e21c17d34a2b8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/02be587bc777b2da1c5d1fb792cb34b5273e4253/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T05:52:00Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T05:52:00Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor: decompose the four worst methods, where tests can prove it (#18)\n\nThe first slice of the decomposition, sliced by **test coverage** rather\nthan by topic — because that is where the risk actually changes.\n\n| Method | Complexity | Coverage | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GeoScore.compute` | **43** | 99% | 8 methods |\n| `RobotsRules.evaluate` | **27** | 98% | 4 methods |\n| `LlmsFreshness.check` | **19** | 100% | 4 methods |\n| `RobotsRules.parse` | **16** | 98% | 5 methods |\n\nSonar CRITICAL findings **81 → 76**; all four targets gone.\n\n## The interesting one\n\nSonar called `compute` a Brain Method with **34 variables**, and the\nvariable count was the disease rather than the length. It opened by\nderiving a dozen facts from the report and then kept every one as a\nlocal, so no group of checks could be lifted out without re-deriving the\ncontrol or re-walking the agents.\n\nExtracting a `Facts` object first made every later extraction trivial.\nThat generalises: when a method resists decomposition, the blocker is\nusually shared state, not size.\n\n`RobotsRules.evaluate` now says what it was already doing — find the\ngroup that names this agent, fall back to `*`, then let the spec''s own\ntie-breaks pick a rule: longest pattern wins, `Allow` wins a tie.\n\n## Behaviour is unchanged, and that is measured\n\nNot asserted:\n\n- **193 unit tests pass unchanged**, including the 59 that pin every\n`GeoScore` check by id, group, severity, verdict and reported value —\nand the exact tally an empty report produces\n- **127/127 Cypress assertions** pass\n\nThis is the payoff from #16 landing first. Without those tests,\nrewriting a 43-complexity scoring engine would have been a change nobody\ncould verify: the Cypress suite asserts that a scan happened and that\nthe UI reads `n of m checks passed`, never which n or which m.\n\n## What is deliberately NOT here\n\nThe eight remaining over-limit methods:\n\n`LinkGraph` 28 · `PageFetch` 26 · `LlmsGenerator` 24 · `Languages` 22 ×2\n· `GuestVisibility` 21 · `PublishedMap` 18 · `ScanStore` 17 ·\n`RobotsEditor` 16\n\nEvery one is JCR-coupled and has **no test**. Decomposing them now would\nbe precisely the mistake this PR exists to avoid — rewriting complex\nlogic with nothing able to prove the output did not move.\n\nThey need characterisation tests first, which needs Mockito or an\nin-memory repository. That is the same body of work as the 80% coverage\nbar, and it should be its own PR rather than smuggled into a refactor.\n\nSequenced after #13, #14, #15, #16 and #17, all merged.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"f61ffa7aaf7e22503cfa877a8e33405d2625e44f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/f61ffa7aaf7e22503cfa877a8e33405d2625e44f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/02be587bc777b2da1c5d1fb792cb34b5273e4253","verification":{"payload":"tree f61ffa7aaf7e22503cfa877a8e33405d2625e44f\nparent cef15bca10176e0f60bfc06bfd09c5942ca80e04\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789537920 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789537920 +0200\n\nrefactor: decompose the four worst methods, where tests can prove it (#18)\n\nThe first slice of the decomposition, sliced by **test coverage** rather\nthan by topic — because that is where the risk actually changes.\n\n| Method | Complexity | Coverage | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GeoScore.compute` | **43** | 99% | 8 methods |\n| `RobotsRules.evaluate` | **27** | 98% | 4 methods |\n| `LlmsFreshness.check` | **19** | 100% | 4 methods |\n| `RobotsRules.parse` | **16** | 98% | 5 methods |\n\nSonar CRITICAL findings **81 → 76**; all four targets gone.\n\n## The interesting one\n\nSonar called `compute` a Brain Method with **34 variables**, and the\nvariable count was the disease rather than the length. It opened by\nderiving a dozen facts from the report and then kept every one as a\nlocal, so no group of checks could be lifted out without re-deriving the\ncontrol or re-walking the agents.\n\nExtracting a `Facts` object first made every later extraction trivial.\nThat generalises: when a method resists decomposition, the blocker is\nusually shared state, not size.\n\n`RobotsRules.evaluate` now says what it was already doing — find the\ngroup that names this agent, fall back to `*`, then let the spec''s own\ntie-breaks pick a rule: longest pattern wins, `Allow` wins a tie.\n\n## Behaviour is unchanged, and that is measured\n\nNot asserted:\n\n- **193 unit tests pass unchanged**, including the 59 that pin every\n`GeoScore` check by id, group, severity, verdict and reported value —\nand the exact tally an empty report produces\n- **127/127 Cypress assertions** pass\n\nThis is the payoff from #16 landing first. Without those tests,\nrewriting a 43-complexity scoring engine would have been a change nobody\ncould verify: the Cypress suite asserts that a scan happened and that\nthe UI reads `n of m checks passed`, never which n or which m.\n\n## What is deliberately NOT here\n\nThe eight remaining over-limit methods:\n\n`LinkGraph` 28 · `PageFetch` 26 · `LlmsGenerator` 24 · `Languages` 22 ×2\n· `GuestVisibility` 21 · `PublishedMap` 18 · `ScanStore` 17 ·\n`RobotsEditor` 16\n\nEvery one is JCR-coupled and has **no test**. Decomposing them now would\nbe precisely the mistake this PR exists to avoid — rewriting complex\nlogic with nothing able to prove the output did not move.\n\nThey need characterisation tests first, which needs Mockito or an\nin-memory repository. That is the same body of work as the 80% coverage\nbar, and it should be its own PR rather than smuggled into a refactor.\n\nSequenced after #13, #14, #15, #16 and #17, all merged.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqi6ACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAEHQQAFZwIESACNXynScZhS4ZKdUx\nSkexJ5qC4JFHRKCiB6re1ESF10jX0neAwDXFrKQoBFhCSEOHIrjB78X/pNZyiFVI\nRg755iQ7eiTQmrOwnBZih40xXaSpU+tDQTs2fqHemXRFzpe8Xct+Ni67h9L5tKp1\nDPL7vf3shuxYhnpPShVq3wW81eJYPIMjoPxYxcyXaZPpoRc7IKWoOZ8kJ2HtI0Gj\nC7mSHckE/8S8+AEi3ne1yexcjr/eD8SE7N1N9SzR5MYD/07DLCK00ExNLqUInHav\nHDmXJ4xFfo4S25epsIk5hNUKEp0LDY/0kMUSrVky+hjUa1RZt+BhTWr7HrwAh+9G\nYDgESfb1DBhg8eE7TK7cTUphoATjXTzt3b5kCXUfH6CyPoWv49Xej3uuRLq1vsE5\nmAvLSkGKX/aqdfzcPkQnn+GAwjVHJo5J6Ih5lxk77KNUS6evdRjq6Ch+Np/qnFmB\nF97Hf3cUnjfNax7ooc7wSiqAEY+dAnkP4fWKPkvBx+Kh8nHhYkECg98UixrqCWKE\nsPTtt4yVdx04L92EJ21EeldP50NLZ4zUirPhmYZqC1TzKm+sk/5XQVoGF6qN2MNJ\n/eKPcbeE3M5jSKMvtamK1/hjJ5tx4HRuhcidkohHEllxGz4uZJCpCO3/SOgt3ECO\nO/VFP7qsIvkk9hCuzLmf\n=qDMh\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T05:52:00Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/02be587bc777b2da1c5d1fb792cb34b5273e4253","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDAyYmU1ODdiYzc3N2IyZGExYzVkMWZiNzkyY2IzNGI1MjczZTQyNTM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/cef15bca10176e0f60bfc06bfd09c5942ca80e04","sha":"cef15bca10176e0f60bfc06bfd09c5942ca80e04","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/cef15bca10176e0f60bfc06bfd09c5942ca80e04"}],"sha":"02be587bc777b2da1c5d1fb792cb34b5273e4253","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/02be587bc777b2da1c5d1fb792cb34b5273e4253"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/cef15bca10176e0f60bfc06bfd09c5942ca80e04/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T05:15:49Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T05:15:49Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: the robots.txt $ anchor, wrong in two opposite directions (#17)\n\n`RobotsRules.matches` got the `$` end-anchor wrong in **two opposite\ndirections**. It chose its branch from the last part of the split on\n`*`, and had both backwards.\n\n## The two defects\n\n```java\nreturn parts[parts.length - 1].isEmpty()\n     ? idx == path.length()                          // taken by ''abc*$''\n     : path.endsWith(parts[parts.length - 1]);       // taken by ''/foo$''\n```\n\n| Pattern | Path | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `/foo$` | `/foo/bar/foo` | `true` ✗ | `false` ✓ |\n| `abc*$` | `abcdef` | `false` ✗ | `true` ✓ |\n| `/a*b$` | `/axbyb` | — | `true` ✓ |\n| `/*.pdf$` | `/report.pdf.html` | `false` ✓ | `false` ✓ |\n\n**`/foo$`** has no `*`, so the last part is the whole pattern. The code\nasked `startsWith` at the front and `endsWith` at the back\n**independently**, never tying them to one occurrence — so any path that\ndid both matched a pattern meaning exactly one.\n\n**`abc*$`** splits to `[\"abc\", \"\"]`, and the empty last part took the\n*ends exactly here* branch — so the trailing `*`, which reads as \"then\nanything\", rejected every path longer than its prefix.\n\n## The fix, and the regression it had to avoid\n\nAn empty final part means the pattern ended with `*`: anything runs to\nthe end. A non-empty one has to sit **at** the end, and there\nspecifically — which is why it is now held back from the forward scan\nrather than matched greedily with the rest.\n\nMatching it greedily would reject `/a*b$` against `/axbyb`: the `b` the\nscan reaches first is at index 3, and it is not the one the anchor is\nabout. That case has its own test.\n\n## How the blast radius was established\n\nThese were found by writing characterisation tests for this class in\n#16, which **pinned both defects rather than hiding them**. Applying the\nfix failed exactly those two tests out of forty-two — and nothing else.\nThat is the whole argument for having written the tests first: it\nlocalised the change to precisely the two cases intended and proved the\nother forty behaviours were untouched.\n\nBoth tests now state the intended meaning, with the old behaviour named\nin a comment, exactly as that file''s own header instructs. A third was\nadded for `/a*b$`.\n\n## Customer-visible\n\nA site whose `robots.txt` uses `$` anchors will get **different\nverdicts** in the drawer''s policy-versus-reality cross-check, and may\nsee its site score move. The new verdicts are the correct ones; the old\nones were not.\n\nNarrow in practice — the common `/*.pdf$` form worked by accident and\nstill works — but real.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **193 unit tests** pass\n- **127/127 Cypress assertions** pass\n\nSequenced after #13, #14, #15 and #16, all merged.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"31dfcaf188bb1d3db636fa5b1fcbde799acd9c7d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/31dfcaf188bb1d3db636fa5b1fcbde799acd9c7d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/cef15bca10176e0f60bfc06bfd09c5942ca80e04","verification":{"payload":"tree 31dfcaf188bb1d3db636fa5b1fcbde799acd9c7d\nparent b9da2231c90d32cc08ddf4aa93434bf49939f0e3\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789535749 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789535749 +0200\n\nfix: the robots.txt $ anchor, wrong in two opposite directions (#17)\n\n`RobotsRules.matches` got the `$` end-anchor wrong in **two opposite\ndirections**. It chose its branch from the last part of the split on\n`*`, and had both backwards.\n\n## The two defects\n\n```java\nreturn parts[parts.length - 1].isEmpty()\n     ? idx == path.length()                          // taken by ''abc*$''\n     : path.endsWith(parts[parts.length - 1]);       // taken by ''/foo$''\n```\n\n| Pattern | Path | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `/foo$` | `/foo/bar/foo` | `true` ✗ | `false` ✓ |\n| `abc*$` | `abcdef` | `false` ✗ | `true` ✓ |\n| `/a*b$` | `/axbyb` | — | `true` ✓ |\n| `/*.pdf$` | `/report.pdf.html` | `false` ✓ | `false` ✓ |\n\n**`/foo$`** has no `*`, so the last part is the whole pattern. The code\nasked `startsWith` at the front and `endsWith` at the back\n**independently**, never tying them to one occurrence — so any path that\ndid both matched a pattern meaning exactly one.\n\n**`abc*$`** splits to `[\"abc\", \"\"]`, and the empty last part took the\n*ends exactly here* branch — so the trailing `*`, which reads as \"then\nanything\", rejected every path longer than its prefix.\n\n## The fix, and the regression it had to avoid\n\nAn empty final part means the pattern ended with `*`: anything runs to\nthe end. A non-empty one has to sit **at** the end, and there\nspecifically — which is why it is now held back from the forward scan\nrather than matched greedily with the rest.\n\nMatching it greedily would reject `/a*b$` against `/axbyb`: the `b` the\nscan reaches first is at index 3, and it is not the one the anchor is\nabout. That case has its own test.\n\n## How the blast radius was established\n\nThese were found by writing characterisation tests for this class in\n#16, which **pinned both defects rather than hiding them**. Applying the\nfix failed exactly those two tests out of forty-two — and nothing else.\nThat is the whole argument for having written the tests first: it\nlocalised the change to precisely the two cases intended and proved the\nother forty behaviours were untouched.\n\nBoth tests now state the intended meaning, with the old behaviour named\nin a comment, exactly as that file''s own header instructs. A third was\nadded for `/a*b$`.\n\n## Customer-visible\n\nA site whose `robots.txt` uses `$` anchors will get **different\nverdicts** in the drawer''s policy-versus-reality cross-check, and may\nsee its site score move. The new verdicts are the correct ones; the old\nones were not.\n\nNarrow in practice — the common `/*.pdf$` form worked by accident and\nstill works — but real.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **193 unit tests** pass\n- **127/127 Cypress assertions** pass\n\nSequenced after #13, #14, #15 and #16, all merged.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqiYFCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAjN8QABvOdvvIP8B821jF3nUp533u\nZxmsRRu6UL40QHP0ddeokBKefS7bxvo7uXef6QZQ3RMAlsu5hoxHSIQJfPKWu4B0\nPNyivgvRAO93QDq2SmIeK2Yq04P3anJYYCRv/o3wh5RQ/H2W0zWd1pRab2heA0vI\nUUdg5emKnOD4JWosrzdnYENLlSFaNk+dOogmwUMMI+kQ/oD/u0ve3Vj9UrAPLKS6\nNGnKQ+RQLR69s9FBOxvHQH6SeH7P5DXIGqIxAJCp4F47yGXy87UIwR/iGRvCxNpZ\n1PDG3JmbxosBmaOdM2Rr5XaoMxbFePFdbNdGdZJOwf+XDdE+8DC11l/03ulBmUVq\nMe5LqRmTPojow0L2ZFRHNLmVIUgZbV1Pe/j8YsVFttOvKPULF8/wmM2pCSLAhC3i\nLGE1i7CKRcZTZGyOjU8++mm4dvp74xnx/LxKfzUM28wtwunf3f/sVOGQZnxEXQK3\nUDS/SAbHApRcyJ1x+ncVQyfFVfO1qNFdpbGlgzNHKNt6IPKn38ZDSWjuArDQDcjZ\niaBQ6JbHifThvZtntGbIOO1xFoTgKGIfaprSR28ynYPUc5AE19cY+buxG2/FII3k\nhf0kpBm8gc74b5gNrmPuFru/UpwF+++JtBw3M4F1EsOHWQRwVP5htRZm+sL1Ic/c\nARDOWXwMAq+/Wf3ZgeCd\n=a8wI\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T05:15:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/cef15bca10176e0f60bfc06bfd09c5942ca80e04","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKGNlZjE1YmNhMTAxNzZlMGY2MGJmYzA2YmZkMDljNTk0MmNhODBlMDQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/b9da2231c90d32cc08ddf4aa93434bf49939f0e3","sha":"b9da2231c90d32cc08ddf4aa93434bf49939f0e3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/b9da2231c90d32cc08ddf4aa93434bf49939f0e3"}],"sha":"cef15bca10176e0f60bfc06bfd09c5942ca80e04","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/cef15bca10176e0f60bfc06bfd09c5942ca80e04"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/b9da2231c90d32cc08ddf4aa93434bf49939f0e3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T22:00:31Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T22:00:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test: characterisation tests before the decomposition (#16)\n\nThe module had **no `src/test` at all**. This adds one, and it goes\nbefore the decomposition rather than after it.\n\n## Why this order\n\nThe next PR decomposes `GeoScore.compute` — cognitive complexity **43**,\nflagged a Brain Method with 34 variables — and both the drawer and the\nsite-wide scan route through it.\n\nNothing pinned its output. The Cypress suite asserts that a run\n*happened* and that the UI reads `n of m checks passed`; it never\nasserts **which** n or **which** m. A refactor that changed every site''s\nscore would have passed the entire suite green.\n\n## What is here\n\n189 tests. JUnit 5 + AssertJ, JaCoCo **reporting, not enforcing** — a\nratchet on a module starting at zero is either set so low it says\nnothing or blocks every unrelated change.\n\n| Class | Coverage | Why this one |\n|---|---|---|\n| `LlmsFreshness` | **100%** | complexity 19, refactor target |\n| `GeoScore` | **99%** | complexity 43, the scoring engine |\n| `RobotsRules` | **98%** | complexity 27/16/16, policy evaluation |\n| `FetchGuard` | 32% | the same-origin guard behind the SSRF |\n| `SiteScope` | 23% | `isLanguage`, the traversal guard |\n\n`FetchGuard` and `SiteScope` are low **on purpose**: their remaining\nmethods resolve DNS and query a JMX MBean server for servlet connector\nports. A test of those would be an environment check, not a unit test.\n\n`LlmsFreshness` pins the **JSON type** of every key it emits, not just\nthe values — because a reader in `GeoReport` already had `stale` and\n`outdated` the wrong way round, so every report ever produced said\n`stale: false` and `0 listed pages that changed since`.\n\n## These are characterisation tests\n\nThey freeze what the code does **today**, not what it ought to do. Where\nbehaviour looks wrong it is pinned anyway with a `SUSPECT:` comment\nnaming the concern, so that the refactor is judged purely on whether it\npreserves behaviour, and the defects are triaged separately on their\nmerits.\n\n## Seven SUSPECT findings, two confirmed real\n\n`RobotsRules.matches` — both verified against the source:\n\n1. **A `$`-anchored pattern with no wildcard checks its start and end\nindependently.** `matches(\"/foo$\", \"/foo/bar/foo\")` returns `true`,\nbecause the code asks `startsWith(\"/foo\")` and `endsWith(\"/foo\")`\nwithout tying them to one occurrence.\n2. **A trailing `*$` is neutered.** `matches(\"abc*$\", \"abcdef\")` returns\n`false` — the split leaves an empty final part, which takes the `idx ==\npath.length()` branch, so the `*` rejects exactly what it should admit.\n\nBoth feed the drawer''s policy-vs-reality cross-check and the site score,\nso a wrong verdict tells an editor the wrong thing. Narrow in practice —\nthe common `/*.pdf$` form works by accident — but real.\n\nThe other five are pinned and commented: an asymmetric empty-check in\n`LlmsFreshness` (`served` is trimmed, `generated` is not), `missing`\ncomputed without consulting the published map, `listedPaths` uncapped\nwhile `stale` and `missing` are capped at 100, `sameContentForCrawlers`\npassing when there was nothing to compare, and blank lines not\nseparating `User-agent` stanzas — that last one may well be correct per\nRFC 9309, which tells crawlers to ignore empty lines, so it needs\nchecking before anyone \"fixes\" it.\n\n## On the 80% coverage bar\n\nOverall line coverage is **8.2%** (336/4104), not 80%. The classes this\nPR targets are at 98–100%; the gap is the JCR-coupled majority —\n`ScanStore`, `PublishedMap`, `GuestVisibility`, `Languages`, `LinkGraph`\nand the servlets — which need Mockito or an in-memory repository before\nthey can be tested at all. That is a substantially larger piece of work\nand it is not in this PR.\n\nNo production code is touched. Sequenced after #13, #14 and #15, all\nmerged.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"06c497f1150bc4b3e3e34d6da80c053f2a3916e2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/06c497f1150bc4b3e3e34d6da80c053f2a3916e2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/b9da2231c90d32cc08ddf4aa93434bf49939f0e3","verification":{"payload":"tree 06c497f1150bc4b3e3e34d6da80c053f2a3916e2\nparent a78c5c2177bd126e5a7d4f934613d3af6cbcad4e\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789509631 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789509631 +0200\n\ntest: characterisation tests before the decomposition (#16)\n\nThe module had **no `src/test` at all**. This adds one, and it goes\nbefore the decomposition rather than after it.\n\n## Why this order\n\nThe next PR decomposes `GeoScore.compute` — cognitive complexity **43**,\nflagged a Brain Method with 34 variables — and both the drawer and the\nsite-wide scan route through it.\n\nNothing pinned its output. The Cypress suite asserts that a run\n*happened* and that the UI reads `n of m checks passed`; it never\nasserts **which** n or **which** m. A refactor that changed every site''s\nscore would have passed the entire suite green.\n\n## What is here\n\n189 tests. JUnit 5 + AssertJ, JaCoCo **reporting, not enforcing** — a\nratchet on a module starting at zero is either set so low it says\nnothing or blocks every unrelated change.\n\n| Class | Coverage | Why this one |\n|---|---|---|\n| `LlmsFreshness` | **100%** | complexity 19, refactor target |\n| `GeoScore` | **99%** | complexity 43, the scoring engine |\n| `RobotsRules` | **98%** | complexity 27/16/16, policy evaluation |\n| `FetchGuard` | 32% | the same-origin guard behind the SSRF |\n| `SiteScope` | 23% | `isLanguage`, the traversal guard |\n\n`FetchGuard` and `SiteScope` are low **on purpose**: their remaining\nmethods resolve DNS and query a JMX MBean server for servlet connector\nports. A test of those would be an environment check, not a unit test.\n\n`LlmsFreshness` pins the **JSON type** of every key it emits, not just\nthe values — because a reader in `GeoReport` already had `stale` and\n`outdated` the wrong way round, so every report ever produced said\n`stale: false` and `0 listed pages that changed since`.\n\n## These are characterisation tests\n\nThey freeze what the code does **today**, not what it ought to do. Where\nbehaviour looks wrong it is pinned anyway with a `SUSPECT:` comment\nnaming the concern, so that the refactor is judged purely on whether it\npreserves behaviour, and the defects are triaged separately on their\nmerits.\n\n## Seven SUSPECT findings, two confirmed real\n\n`RobotsRules.matches` — both verified against the source:\n\n1. **A `$`-anchored pattern with no wildcard checks its start and end\nindependently.** `matches(\"/foo$\", \"/foo/bar/foo\")` returns `true`,\nbecause the code asks `startsWith(\"/foo\")` and `endsWith(\"/foo\")`\nwithout tying them to one occurrence.\n2. **A trailing `*$` is neutered.** `matches(\"abc*$\", \"abcdef\")` returns\n`false` — the split leaves an empty final part, which takes the `idx ==\npath.length()` branch, so the `*` rejects exactly what it should admit.\n\nBoth feed the drawer''s policy-vs-reality cross-check and the site score,\nso a wrong verdict tells an editor the wrong thing. Narrow in practice —\nthe common `/*.pdf$` form works by accident — but real.\n\nThe other five are pinned and commented: an asymmetric empty-check in\n`LlmsFreshness` (`served` is trimmed, `generated` is not), `missing`\ncomputed without consulting the published map, `listedPaths` uncapped\nwhile `stale` and `missing` are capped at 100, `sameContentForCrawlers`\npassing when there was nothing to compare, and blank lines not\nseparating `User-agent` stanzas — that last one may well be correct per\nRFC 9309, which tells crawlers to ignore empty lines, so it needs\nchecking before anyone \"fixes\" it.\n\n## On the 80% coverage bar\n\nOverall line coverage is **8.2%** (336/4104), not 80%. The classes this\nPR targets are at 98–100%; the gap is the JCR-coupled majority —\n`ScanStore`, `PublishedMap`, `GuestVisibility`, `Languages`, `LinkGraph`\nand the servlets — which need Mockito or an in-memory repository before\nthey can be tested at all. That is a substantially larger piece of work\nand it is not in this PR.\n\nNo production code is touched. Sequenced after #13, #14 and #15, all\nmerged.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqb//CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAqT4QAK2OEwL1UjtY8u4GaEw5KIoa\nPt3ch761O066XGvh4NOECg3Gj3wAvW1/RBul8s33t1wofZjuq/DN6rCt1rskLEXK\nlOSAroWUmfPDNecDDGjuZ1e/ClMy0Uw47uSkkaVrirZvkPmDtg022pEZGuhf90eu\n7gXtINaJKK6C9D2RF/2uL5EieAunFU9RI+JEjfgTBfv1mvDvhee9D3CqkiGRHZ6p\n7UKCSx3lMT0nB6O56c2hVvo/dI1lTBkp+fJe4/uZ0dyYqNSnFG72EMhLglyO9sUv\nSkyrE/JqAbLhBWwlAt+HW2BzLS93JtaD1VyQLSVR0huwgO6mbbr8cDelrSA5ACLu\nZnldE1XFg9l9ekMXxpjRJyTEP8qsVt1J+j5vrFEAQethmddV86T/F88RF+C8cHDc\nsce5i82kTyqd9xiHcn89ncm3UFAg95kXMoym+NbzN8IRAutUtfgE9D/y8GMQv48D\n6Op8cjnCeyU1DSKMUnJQ83cSY2PhIaKT7mLC1v7yqTfxAJSMmcEOqsKjpIqdFJSE\n2Z9TwrOI/7R3L0vcdUMevhBNnjaSdzApWSwlc/3pAdANwlHCJfndtLdyYH8OPcd4\nGBeUpwqth5XZekv8WIpCs77npLuV5sNrkgbaMJV81yaNe/Y3aPF5dZG8OPHRnd/v\nPMHjroIuAniOF+iyTACJ\n=cQem\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T22:00:32Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/b9da2231c90d32cc08ddf4aa93434bf49939f0e3","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKGI5ZGEyMjMxYzkwZDMyY2MwOGRkZjRhYTkzNDM0YmY0OTkzOWYwZTM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/a78c5c2177bd126e5a7d4f934613d3af6cbcad4e","sha":"a78c5c2177bd126e5a7d4f934613d3af6cbcad4e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/a78c5c2177bd126e5a7d4f934613d3af6cbcad4e"}],"sha":"b9da2231c90d32cc08ddf4aa93434bf49939f0e3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/b9da2231c90d32cc08ddf4aa93434bf49939f0e3"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/a78c5c2177bd126e5a7d4f934613d3af6cbcad4e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T21:18:43Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T21:18:43Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: take the base url host from the repository, not from the request (#15)\n\n`main`''s own quality gate was **ERROR** after #13 and #14 merged, on\n`software_quality_security_rating: 3`. One finding was responsible —\n`javasecurity:S5144` on `FetchGuard`''s `openConnection()`. This is the\nfix, and it is a real defect rather than a false positive.\n\n## The gap between the comment and the code\n\n`PublicUrls.derived` has said this all along:\n\n> The host is the site''s own, always: the request only gets to say WHICH\nof the site''s names is in use, never a name of its own.\n\nIt did the *check* that way and then built the url the other way.\n`servedBy` compared the request''s host against the site''s names with\n`equalsIgnoreCase` and returned a **boolean**; `onThisJahia` then\nassembled the url from `req.getServerName()` — the request''s copy of the\nstring.\n\n## Why that is not cosmetic\n\nEqual under `equalsIgnoreCase` is not identical:\n\n```\nHost: WWW.ACME.COM   matches   site.serverName = www.acme.com\n                     produces  base = https://WWW.ACME.COM\n```\n\n`SiteScanServlet` **persists that base in `geoBaseUrl`**, so a casing\nthe caller chose outlived the request and was reused by every scheduled\nscan afterwards. It also left the one string in the url that a request\ncould still influence — the far end of which is the `openConnection()`\nSonar flagged.\n\n## The change\n\n`servedBy` becomes `servedName` and returns the **repository''s**\nspelling of whichever name matched. The request still *selects*; it no\nlonger *supplies*.\n\nNothing request-controlled reaches the url now:\n\n| Part | Source |\n|---|---|\n| host | `site.getServerName()` or a `serverNameAlias` |\n| port | `site.url.port`, else the socket''s local port |\n| scheme | `req.isSecure()` |\n\n## Verification\n\n| | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| `software_quality_security_rating` | 3 (C) | **1 (A)** |\n| `javasecurity:S5144` | 1 | **0** |\n| Project gate | ERROR | **OK** |\n\n**127/127 Cypress assertions pass.** This base feeds every crawler\nfetch, so that is the check that matters.\n\nSequenced after #13 and #14, both merged. Deliberately kept separate\nfrom the code-quality refactor so a security correction is not queued\nbehind a large decomposition.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"ad246c0e771d9df35574cadde538b4c4bac9f800","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/ad246c0e771d9df35574cadde538b4c4bac9f800"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/a78c5c2177bd126e5a7d4f934613d3af6cbcad4e","verification":{"payload":"tree ad246c0e771d9df35574cadde538b4c4bac9f800\nparent 7ade10b6ee3832f1dde77d85b07aa3c51c9f39a6\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789507123 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789507123 +0200\n\nfix: take the base url host from the repository, not from the request (#15)\n\n`main`''s own quality gate was **ERROR** after #13 and #14 merged, on\n`software_quality_security_rating: 3`. One finding was responsible —\n`javasecurity:S5144` on `FetchGuard`''s `openConnection()`. This is the\nfix, and it is a real defect rather than a false positive.\n\n## The gap between the comment and the code\n\n`PublicUrls.derived` has said this all along:\n\n> The host is the site''s own, always: the request only gets to say WHICH\nof the site''s names is in use, never a name of its own.\n\nIt did the *check* that way and then built the url the other way.\n`servedBy` compared the request''s host against the site''s names with\n`equalsIgnoreCase` and returned a **boolean**; `onThisJahia` then\nassembled the url from `req.getServerName()` — the request''s copy of the\nstring.\n\n## Why that is not cosmetic\n\nEqual under `equalsIgnoreCase` is not identical:\n\n```\nHost: WWW.ACME.COM   matches   site.serverName = www.acme.com\n                     produces  base = https://WWW.ACME.COM\n```\n\n`SiteScanServlet` **persists that base in `geoBaseUrl`**, so a casing\nthe caller chose outlived the request and was reused by every scheduled\nscan afterwards. It also left the one string in the url that a request\ncould still influence — the far end of which is the `openConnection()`\nSonar flagged.\n\n## The change\n\n`servedBy` becomes `servedName` and returns the **repository''s**\nspelling of whichever name matched. The request still *selects*; it no\nlonger *supplies*.\n\nNothing request-controlled reaches the url now:\n\n| Part | Source |\n|---|---|\n| host | `site.getServerName()` or a `serverNameAlias` |\n| port | `site.url.port`, else the socket''s local port |\n| scheme | `req.isSecure()` |\n\n## Verification\n\n| | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| `software_quality_security_rating` | 3 (C) | **1 (A)** |\n| `javasecurity:S5144` | 1 | **0** |\n| Project gate | ERROR | **OK** |\n\n**127/127 Cypress assertions pass.** This base feeds every crawler\nfetch, so that is the check that matters.\n\nSequenced after #13 and #14, both merged. Deliberately kept separate\nfrom the code-quality refactor so a security correction is not queued\nbehind a large decomposition.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqbYzCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAHd4QAAUbVOuOdP+eAVLlbHyD8kl1\niQUcVgObCIyBgT9+LUOC6na8cxPSw3BNCziNdMbzMwqmCKzl/sq3NAUp1KLCuRZQ\ngcYeLPuz+iHSthERW9YAMXiKG77eNvSqupfaGQT4RfirdeIRWnE+n/iwUgKq9TUZ\n3rim3TnIt/2cHWzLWhDNc4eAS4ipMr4ZFB4i6flDf6V6ipLYwcmW/j8366ouOBSm\nfFqAxxhHi3K+G2QCn2OssBQVc0kVEfOs7GLj/cdN0wuVt2fDZtFZeRdqLJKLL9tf\nXjbqRRfILq7oOfLyN2hrIaSTb0AoICRo5s8mJrkR8UVqPpn9FB94JD/rn6SWN3GV\nmIZrVvUsmIzleB/JypRK/VtcPUv41J8izEVq/gXSUghXE2qqlbHN7w4rZ8gekk6m\nyRh2VTXLKdn6twXqesvYoUwWOnpOox+551U/sou/32NJTqikberefPXA8jF1Y7Pw\npTZgauffzT1KPayhzRtIZeLF7P10+6MPPsg1ibhknONcS6ARoA7fumJa5E3b8ayH\nKvZIwfyW25fqUSGa1szneM9QGqv9DZ5jcDD/HMDGYiccQ+jQzGF7SSSLKrrB/aCD\n4XV9ekqYMdMKH7dFgfPrOeIJxgtSVzg/VCZmwMwh1mhQKc7LMCkShiXZ60BcgiTi\ng9V3V/f0BPWoqk4alJG0\n=nqcU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T21:18:44Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/a78c5c2177bd126e5a7d4f934613d3af6cbcad4e","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKGE3OGM1YzIxNzdiZDEyNmU1YTdkNGY5MzQ2MTNkM2FmNmNiY2FkNGU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/7ade10b6ee3832f1dde77d85b07aa3c51c9f39a6","sha":"7ade10b6ee3832f1dde77d85b07aa3c51c9f39a6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/7ade10b6ee3832f1dde77d85b07aa3c51c9f39a6"}],"sha":"a78c5c2177bd126e5a7d4f934613d3af6cbcad4e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/a78c5c2177bd126e5a7d4f934613d3af6cbcad4e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/7ade10b6ee3832f1dde77d85b07aa3c51c9f39a6/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T20:53:14Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T20:53:14Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: make the interface usable without sight, a mouse, or colour (#14)\n\nMeasured against **WCAG 2.2 Level AAA**. Five of these failed plain AA.\n\n## Contrast\n\n`--geo-ink3` is the default secondary-text colour for every chart — row\nsublabels, axes, legends, matrix and histogram labels — and sat at\n**4.03:1**. It resolved to Moonstone''s gray token, so the token is\n*replaced* rather than kept as a fallback: keeping it would have left\nthe failure in place wherever the token resolves. Now **8.45:1**.\n\nThe keyboard focus ring was `#63d1ff` at **1.74:1** against white — and\nit was the only focus cue on chart tracks and matrix cells. Now\n**5.19:1**.\n\nVerdict badges, crawler marks and links were between 2.66:1 and 5.69:1,\nall now above **7:1**. Every colour was solved for the target luminance,\nnot eyeballed.\n\n## Headings\n\nThere were none. Thirty-three `Typography variant=heading|subheading`\nuses — and Moonstone''s `variant` is *styling*, it does not choose the\nelement — so a screen reader pressing <kbd>H</kbd> found nothing\nanywhere in the module. All thirty-three now carry `component=\"h2\"` or\n`h3` in a real hierarchy.\n\n## Live regions\n\nThere was one `role=\"status\"` in the module, on the chart tooltip, and\nit was mounted already populated each time — the case screen readers\nroutinely do not announce. A site scan runs for minutes and told nobody\nit had started, progressed, finished or failed. The tooltip is one\npersistent node now, and the scan''s progress, failure and error banners\nare live regions.\n\n## Accessible names\n\n- `FailureMatrix` cells were focusable spans with no text, severity\ncarried by background colour alone: on a large scan, **hundreds of\nunnamed tab stops**.\n- `StackedBar`''s container was `role=\"img\"` with no name — which\nflattens its subtree — while the segments inside stayed focusable and\nwere unlabelled below a 12% share.\n- Moonstone''s `Field` renders its label as a sibling with no `htmlFor`,\nso the schedule switch, the cron dropdowns, the scope input and the\nllms.txt textarea had no accessible name either.\n\nAll named now.\n\n## Also\n\nTab panels are associated with their tabs; the drawer moves focus in on\nopen and restores it on close, with <kbd>Esc</kbd> to close; hit areas\nare at least 24×24; chart transitions respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n\n## Not reached, deliberately\n\nAAA''s **2.5.5** 44×44 target size for the matrix cell and the paired\nbars. Widening every column makes a check-heavy matrix unusable, so both\nstop at the **2.5.8** 24×24 floor and say so in the CSS. Moonstone''s own\n`Switch` is 38×20 and is left alone as a third-party component.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Seven new strings, in both bundles; parity holds at **588 keys each**.\n- **127/127 Cypress assertions pass**, including the four that drive the\ndrawer this change adds a focus trap to.\n- Builds clean on JDK 17.\n\nSequenced after #13, which is merged. Cherry-picked onto current `main`\nrather than rebased, because #13 was squash-merged.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"9c02384031c1869ebc075fc8353364bd6ad40aaa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/9c02384031c1869ebc075fc8353364bd6ad40aaa"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/7ade10b6ee3832f1dde77d85b07aa3c51c9f39a6","verification":{"payload":"tree 9c02384031c1869ebc075fc8353364bd6ad40aaa\nparent d276f315fde77248dc47f360866f2ae24564d2cf\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789505594 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789505594 +0200\n\nfix: make the interface usable without sight, a mouse, or colour (#14)\n\nMeasured against **WCAG 2.2 Level AAA**. Five of these failed plain AA.\n\n## Contrast\n\n`--geo-ink3` is the default secondary-text colour for every chart — row\nsublabels, axes, legends, matrix and histogram labels — and sat at\n**4.03:1**. It resolved to Moonstone''s gray token, so the token is\n*replaced* rather than kept as a fallback: keeping it would have left\nthe failure in place wherever the token resolves. Now **8.45:1**.\n\nThe keyboard focus ring was `#63d1ff` at **1.74:1** against white — and\nit was the only focus cue on chart tracks and matrix cells. Now\n**5.19:1**.\n\nVerdict badges, crawler marks and links were between 2.66:1 and 5.69:1,\nall now above **7:1**. Every colour was solved for the target luminance,\nnot eyeballed.\n\n## Headings\n\nThere were none. Thirty-three `Typography variant=heading|subheading`\nuses — and Moonstone''s `variant` is *styling*, it does not choose the\nelement — so a screen reader pressing <kbd>H</kbd> found nothing\nanywhere in the module. All thirty-three now carry `component=\"h2\"` or\n`h3` in a real hierarchy.\n\n## Live regions\n\nThere was one `role=\"status\"` in the module, on the chart tooltip, and\nit was mounted already populated each time — the case screen readers\nroutinely do not announce. A site scan runs for minutes and told nobody\nit had started, progressed, finished or failed. The tooltip is one\npersistent node now, and the scan''s progress, failure and error banners\nare live regions.\n\n## Accessible names\n\n- `FailureMatrix` cells were focusable spans with no text, severity\ncarried by background colour alone: on a large scan, **hundreds of\nunnamed tab stops**.\n- `StackedBar`''s container was `role=\"img\"` with no name — which\nflattens its subtree — while the segments inside stayed focusable and\nwere unlabelled below a 12% share.\n- Moonstone''s `Field` renders its label as a sibling with no `htmlFor`,\nso the schedule switch, the cron dropdowns, the scope input and the\nllms.txt textarea had no accessible name either.\n\nAll named now.\n\n## Also\n\nTab panels are associated with their tabs; the drawer moves focus in on\nopen and restores it on close, with <kbd>Esc</kbd> to close; hit areas\nare at least 24×24; chart transitions respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n\n## Not reached, deliberately\n\nAAA''s **2.5.5** 44×44 target size for the matrix cell and the paired\nbars. Widening every column makes a check-heavy matrix unusable, so both\nstop at the **2.5.8** 24×24 floor and say so in the CSS. Moonstone''s own\n`Switch` is 38×20 and is left alone as a third-party component.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Seven new strings, in both bundles; parity holds at **588 keys each**.\n- **127/127 Cypress assertions pass**, including the four that drive the\ndrawer this change adds a focus trap to.\n- Builds clean on JDK 17.\n\nSequenced after #13, which is merged. Cherry-picked onto current `main`\nrather than rebased, because #13 was squash-merged.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqbA6CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAd2gQAJPZxcIb2q6jKB6v+qBwJj8n\nU1gbM3sBXtpKpYJyMVSH26Q0PF31Y/R4Zeq3pOYiPrBaQTnoZg4t3Z1bnxj3s5RG\nkP0IrxB/ffjaqqi25SRxlfuEEOi7XYsvkIHu9EHDPqgwuyA01+vEs47hNRMzRthT\n/Vwh+8wRv8AhD8WFLxIPNHW1ZjV8QwbL/wnu67Y6oF/pa48CE+L++J35a/Ggr50x\nAGk1tkaYOJTnPqCZEsk0AYTp1yUZFg2xMH/5jQ+agcquXmZQKjA2H16UySsOSgZi\ngJ1+UWgp9yweXO8L+JHOL390ClANczZccTN6kOE0xkfKfksmFSiervlPXV7/J+pE\nluNWMLZKU4bExEwFp9M45ymGqbb+gn+raVHeAJdajeMzA8gqjiNpu7FCak0ZjiWa\nEd4ihWdx4LlhgfDhpXnT9woJ8zd5FDaX6dc8ya/oCzCfgK6tsSyKkzikpyrZhXz2\nkE/sYVfIUArItzbJl3o4bcOFuQAdxuSWXyITb/U8pbIVaZvUtAyO/Ba8z672BFjL\nrSalwXNqWIUxhL0HG55Le/PzNqff2mS/ZIpyzYL36BdkBof/srAOWFr0B+gT09uY\nxCi1CKzu9hSPzJ0WwqwVUvBcAzzeNp9j8M00hSg3cdGmzkW1900J8LvwIpOpZLEe\nQeqbKkbsSt2v6zukdI6e\n=kVUN\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T20:53:15Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/7ade10b6ee3832f1dde77d85b07aa3c51c9f39a6","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDdhZGUxMGI2ZWUzODMyZjFkZGU3N2Q4NWIwN2FhM2M1MWM5ZjM5YTY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/d276f315fde77248dc47f360866f2ae24564d2cf","sha":"d276f315fde77248dc47f360866f2ae24564d2cf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/d276f315fde77248dc47f360866f2ae24564d2cf"}],"sha":"7ade10b6ee3832f1dde77d85b07aa3c51c9f39a6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/7ade10b6ee3832f1dde77d85b07aa3c51c9f39a6"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/d276f315fde77248dc47f360866f2ae24564d2cf/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T20:05:57Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T20:05:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: close the SSRF, gate the ungated endpoint, and six defects where code contradicted its comment (#13)\n\n## Summary\n\nPR 1 of 5, from a seven-dimension blind review of the module\n(architecture, security, code quality, ergonomics, accessibility AAA,\ntest coverage, documentation). This one carries **security and\ncorrectness**; accessibility, the quality gate, tests and documentation\nfollow in their own PRs.\n\n**127/127 Cypress assertions pass** against a real instance with the\nwhole branch applied.\n\n## Security\n\n### SSRF — the request chose where the server connected\n\n`PublicUrls.base()` took the port from `req.getServerPort()`, which per\nthe Servlet spec is the part after `:` in the **Host header**.\n`servedBy()` validated the host against the site''s serverName and\naliases but never the port, so `Host: www.acme.com:6379` passed and\nproduced the base `http://www.acme.com:6379`.\n\nThe module then made **19 outbound requests** there — 16 crawler agents\nplus `robots.txt`, `llms.txt`, `llms-full.txt` — and returned, per\nagent: status, timing, byte count, the `Location` header, the exception\n**class name** (`ConnectException` vs `SocketTimeoutException`, a clean\nopen/closed/filtered oracle) and the parsed title, h1, word count and\nJSON-LD types of anything that answered. An authenticated internal port\nscanner with response reflection. `saveSchedule` then **persisted** that\nbase into `geoBaseUrl`, so every later scheduled scan reused it.\n\n`FetchGuard` could not catch it: in `CrawlerCheckServlet` the guard''s\n`base` is re-derived from `publicUrl` itself, making the same-origin\ncheck tautological there.\n\nNow: the port comes from `site.url.port` or `req.getLocalPort()` — the\nsocket the request arrived on, which no header can move — and the scheme\nfrom `req.isSecure()`. Defence in depth in `FetchGuard`: a port\nallowlist, and rejection of loopback, wildcard, link-local, site-local,\nunique-local and multicast across **every** address a name resolves to.\n`PUBLIC_BASE_URL` and the `localhost:8080` fallback stay trusted, both\nbeing server-side decisions.\n\n### The one endpoint with no permission gate\n\n`CrawlerCheckServlet` had zero `SiteScope.require` calls; the other\nthree servlets have one or two. It was the low-privilege entry to the\nSSRF above, and its six enrichment blocks read stored scan data through\n`ScanStore` under a **system session** — whose own comment says \"Reading\nthem is gated at the servlet instead\". It was not. A user with read on\none page obtained the site''s overall score, per-section scores and\nper-template failure statistics for a site they held no dashboard\npermission on.\n\nThe per-page check stays reachable by any editor, which is the product\nintent. Only the stored-scan enrichments are gated, and they are\n**omitted** rather than erroring — the frontend already renders on\nabsence.\n\n## Correctness — six defects where the code contradicted its own comment\n\n- **`.cfg` protection marker misspelled** `overriden`. Jahia''s extender\nwants `overridden` exactly, so the deployed `karaf/etc` file was\nrewritten on every module start and the administrator''s `AI_API_KEY` and\n`PUBLIC_BASE_URL` went with it — while line 6 of that same file promised\nthe opposite. The typo was also **enshrined as a hard invariant in\n`.agents/README.md`**, which is how it survived: the agent guide taught\nit forward. Both fixed.\n- **`ScanLifecycle`** guarded `activate()` on `isProcessingServer()` and\nleft `deactivate()` open. The scheduler is Jahia''s persistent,\ncluster-shared Quartz store, so any browsing node restarting deleted the\ntriggers for **every site** from the whole cluster — and could not\nreinstall them, because `activate()` returns early on that node. Nightly\nscans stopped silently.\n- **`Freshness`** promised undated items first, in its own comment and\nin three customer-facing documents. Undated items carry `days = -1`, the\nsmallest value, and the sort was descending — so they landed last, and\nthe `MAX_ITEMS` cap kept the first 5000. On a large site the pages with\nno date at all were exactly the ones dropped.\n- **`GeoReport`** read `fromPages` under a heading promising\n`fromTemplate` — `TemplateRollup`''s opposite halves. The AI digest was\nhanded the page-level noise and never saw the roll-up.\n- **`GeoReport`** also read llms `stale` as a boolean and `outdated` as\nan array; `LlmsFreshness` stores them the other way round. Every report\never produced said `stale: false` and `0 listed pages that changed\nsince`, whatever the scan found.\n- **An unpublished page rendered a blank drawer.** The tab bar is gated\non `published`, `tab` starts at `score`, and the only component that\nexplains why was reachable solely through the bar that had just been\nhidden. The message written for it was unreachable and\n`error.notPublished` was referenced nowhere.\n- **`applySiteFile` answered 200 with an error body** — the one refusal\nin the module without a matching status, and the frontend''s `call()`\nhelper only checks `res.ok`, so an empty write was reported as a\nsuccess. Now 400. The Cypress test that deliberately pinned that defect\nnow asserts the corrected contract.\n\n## Concurrency\n\nNothing checked whether a scan was already running. A double-click, or a\ncron trigger firing during a manual run, started two over the same\nstored state: the second copied the first run''s half-written aggregate\ninto `PREVIOUS`, both raced on `progress()` and `finishRun()`, and\n`pagesDone` walked backwards in the UI.\n\n`tryBeginRun` does read, decide and write in one `inStore` callback and\none `session.save()` — a single JCR transaction. A JVM-local flag would\nnot do, since the cron job and the manual run can land on different\ncluster nodes.\n\n**The guard alone would have been worse than the race.**\n`STATUS_RUNNING` is only cleared by the scanning thread reaching\n`finishRun`/`failRun`, so a JVM kill leaves it set for ever and the\nguard would refuse every future scan with no way to clear it from the\nUI. A run older than six hours is treated as abandoned and `read()`\nreports it as failed, so the dashboard stops polling a banner that will\nnever move.\n\nThe servlet answers **409** (busy, not broken); the scheduled job logs\nand steps aside. Neither calls `failRun` on a run it does not own any\nmore — the servlet previously destroyed the state of a scan still in\nflight.\n\n## Deployment notes — two intentional behaviour changes\n\n1. **A reverse proxy publishing a different port**, with neither\n`site.url.port` nor `PUBLIC_BASE_URL` set, now gets the container''s port\nrather than the proxy''s. The Host header used to supply it, which is\nprecisely the vulnerability. Such deployments must set one of the two.\n2. **A site that genuinely answers on a private address** (split-horizon\nDNS, a dev host on 127.0.0.1) is now refused unless `PUBLIC_BASE_URL`\nnames it. `http://localhost:8080` keeps working.\n\n**Operational:** `geoBaseUrl` values already stored on existing\nschedules are not purged. They are neutralised at fetch time — an odd\nport fails the allowlist, a private host fails the address check — but\nan operator who suspects abuse should inspect them.\n\n## Known and not fixed here\n\n- **DNS rebinding.** `requireAllowedTarget` resolves, then\n`openConnection` resolves again. Closing it means connecting by IP and\nforging `Host`, which breaks TLS and virtual hosting — a deliberate\ntrade against an authenticated editor rather than the open internet,\ndocumented in the method.\n- **CGNAT `100.64.0.0/10`** is not rejected; Java has no predicate and\nit is not a container-internal range.\n- **No unit tests.** There is no `src/test/java` in this module at all —\nthat is PR 4. The new address predicates were verified with a standalone\nharness against 127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, 10/172.16/192.168, 169.254,\n224.0.0.1, ::1, fd00::1, fe80::1 (refused) and 8.8.8.8, 93.184.216.34\n(allowed).\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"d4856b5d3f965bc7585e63c08200178a4ffc8d42","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/d4856b5d3f965bc7585e63c08200178a4ffc8d42"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/d276f315fde77248dc47f360866f2ae24564d2cf","verification":{"payload":"tree d4856b5d3f965bc7585e63c08200178a4ffc8d42\nparent efb129acd22ea9d1ab403d66dfe429a0262d60e9\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789502757 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789502757 +0200\n\nfix: close the SSRF, gate the ungated endpoint, and six defects where code contradicted its comment (#13)\n\n## Summary\n\nPR 1 of 5, from a seven-dimension blind review of the module\n(architecture, security, code quality, ergonomics, accessibility AAA,\ntest coverage, documentation). This one carries **security and\ncorrectness**; accessibility, the quality gate, tests and documentation\nfollow in their own PRs.\n\n**127/127 Cypress assertions pass** against a real instance with the\nwhole branch applied.\n\n## Security\n\n### SSRF — the request chose where the server connected\n\n`PublicUrls.base()` took the port from `req.getServerPort()`, which per\nthe Servlet spec is the part after `:` in the **Host header**.\n`servedBy()` validated the host against the site''s serverName and\naliases but never the port, so `Host: www.acme.com:6379` passed and\nproduced the base `http://www.acme.com:6379`.\n\nThe module then made **19 outbound requests** there — 16 crawler agents\nplus `robots.txt`, `llms.txt`, `llms-full.txt` — and returned, per\nagent: status, timing, byte count, the `Location` header, the exception\n**class name** (`ConnectException` vs `SocketTimeoutException`, a clean\nopen/closed/filtered oracle) and the parsed title, h1, word count and\nJSON-LD types of anything that answered. An authenticated internal port\nscanner with response reflection. `saveSchedule`