uide taught\nit forward. Both fixed.\n- **`ScanLifecycle`** guarded `activate()` on `isProcessingServer()` and\nleft `deactivate()` open. The scheduler is Jahia''s persistent,\ncluster-shared Quartz store, so any browsing node restarting deleted the\ntriggers for **every site** from the whole cluster — and could not\nreinstall them, because `activate()` returns early on that node. Nightly\nscans stopped silently.\n- **`Freshness`** promised undated items first, in its own comment and\nin three customer-facing documents. Undated items carry `days = -1`, the\nsmallest value, and the sort was descending — so they landed last, and\nthe `MAX_ITEMS` cap kept the first 5000. On a large site the pages with\nno date at all were exactly the ones dropped.\n- **`GeoReport`** read `fromPages` under a heading promising\n`fromTemplate` — `TemplateRollup`''s opposite halves. The AI digest was\nhanded the page-level noise and never saw the roll-up.\n- **`GeoReport`** also read llms `stale` as a boolean and `outdated` as\nan array; `LlmsFreshness` stores them the other way round. Every report\never produced said `stale: false` and `0 listed pages that changed\nsince`, whatever the scan found.\n- **An unpublished page rendered a blank drawer.** The tab bar is gated\non `published`, `tab` starts at `score`, and the only component that\nexplains why was reachable solely through the bar that had just been\nhidden. The message written for it was unreachable and\n`error.notPublished` was referenced nowhere.\n- **`applySiteFile` answered 200 with an error body** — the one refusal\nin the module without a matching status, and the frontend''s `call()`\nhelper only checks `res.ok`, so an empty write was reported as a\nsuccess. Now 400. The Cypress test that deliberately pinned that defect\nnow asserts the corrected contract.\n\n## Concurrency\n\nNothing checked whether a scan was already running. A double-click, or a\ncron trigger firing during a manual run, started two over the same\nstored state: the second copied the first run''s half-written aggregate\ninto `PREVIOUS`, both raced on `progress()` and `finishRun()`, and\n`pagesDone` walked backwards in the UI.\n\n`tryBeginRun` does read, decide and write in one `inStore` callback and\none `session.save()` — a single JCR transaction. A JVM-local flag would\nnot do, since the cron job and the manual run can land on different\ncluster nodes.\n\n**The guard alone would have been worse than the race.**\n`STATUS_RUNNING` is only cleared by the scanning thread reaching\n`finishRun`/`failRun`, so a JVM kill leaves it set for ever and the\nguard would refuse every future scan with no way to clear it from the\nUI. A run older than six hours is treated as abandoned and `read()`\nreports it as failed, so the dashboard stops polling a banner that will\nnever move.\n\nThe servlet answers **409** (busy, not broken); the scheduled job logs\nand steps aside. Neither calls `failRun` on a run it does not own any\nmore — the servlet previously destroyed the state of a scan still in\nflight.\n\n## Deployment notes — two intentional behaviour changes\n\n1. **A reverse proxy publishing a different port**, with neither\n`site.url.port` nor `PUBLIC_BASE_URL` set, now gets the container''s port\nrather than the proxy''s. The Host header used to supply it, which is\nprecisely the vulnerability. Such deployments must set one of the two.\n2. **A site that genuinely answers on a private address** (split-horizon\nDNS, a dev host on 127.0.0.1) is now refused unless `PUBLIC_BASE_URL`\nnames it. `http://localhost:8080` keeps working.\n\n**Operational:** `geoBaseUrl` values already stored on existing\nschedules are not purged. They are neutralised at fetch time — an odd\nport fails the allowlist, a private host fails the address check — but\nan operator who suspects abuse should inspect them.\n\n## Known and not fixed here\n\n- **DNS rebinding.** `requireAllowedTarget` resolves, then\n`openConnection` resolves again. Closing it means connecting by IP and\nforging `Host`, which breaks TLS and virtual hosting — a deliberate\ntrade against an authenticated editor rather than the open internet,\ndocumented in the method.\n- **CGNAT `100.64.0.0/10`** is not rejected; Java has no predicate and\nit is not a container-internal range.\n- **No unit tests.** There is no `src/test/java` in this module at all —\nthat is PR 4. The new address predicates were verified with a standalone\nharness against 127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, 10/172.16/192.168, 169.254,\n224.0.0.1, ::1, fd00::1, fe80::1 (refused) and 8.8.8.8, 93.184.216.34\n(allowed).\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqaUlCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/3wQAHuSbmUSnV5U5LE57uJ+j2/F\naxGwPJXt6pFmSjqehTRK0aIdpwRBzp64WfJfjICof4+Q2gP+d+CyeQXkvaIqlKgk\nxtjyzM1qGq4y1bmlb9FXG+wEYltNDfONXhqSmbX0fOScWvL28CKRsgk5e5JmXcmr\nB4VIrglnfWg8FOfPXzYI0WVKQb4DOuQyGJ5MiFtK/uZAOZzO1v2aU9XVnt/vdDYM\njiEdw4j3Vvcs/l6ahgwsenjdNuV1CIcJW//t6TBDeV6iLmgmJwx1g8At07HQ7rZL\nAGmoHqXNacKAcCRuNfGKQN/7diluqvBZgPdzPo0DBh6msmjN5m+5OYLTVbkCL6t/\nJ1aOFb/cWNDQYtxY2fDYePMntuq4Xvsqy1Scr6AIi6Dk5PffRRsYgXb3kXK6Ni+V\nZDwbV0UDmwjJRoZ8V3cMI1fV6Ho4co6uKF1V03IHjDumn+u0bW9czKUogOEhw5mz\nLgFJ0x+xc/XUsZ7FjlyCB90wctPJ4yCU/RE+Jm9kxm/Jo7kdWLpc/W+8ZBChP/in\n2ovDkuoStAsK/Skvi2WORXaogiFgDMUfG0JN3Vs0YSFJYKWJYti1+X5ZKiMpvqel\njWw6IrzXTNCtYar+wl3MnLU4SkBqofZ8UahtXkr67GrNzuebtyOGwqsOfm2EhXW3\nvQFqthy5Vu0pmC5348zW\n=C1mF\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T20:05:58Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/d276f315fde77248dc47f360866f2ae24564d2cf","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKGQyNzZmMzE1ZmRlNzcyNDhkYzQ3ZjM2MDg2NmYyYWUyNDU2NGQyY2Y","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/efb129acd22ea9d1ab403d66dfe429a0262d60e9","sha":"efb129acd22ea9d1ab403d66dfe429a0262d60e9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/efb129acd22ea9d1ab403d66dfe429a0262d60e9"}],"sha":"d276f315fde77248dc47f360866f2ae24564d2cf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/d276f315fde77248dc47f360866f2ae24564d2cf"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/efb129acd22ea9d1ab403d66dfe429a0262d60e9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T16:11:01Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T16:11:01Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test: make the e2e suite runnable, green, and wired to CI (#12)\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `tests/` Cypress suite went from **0 passing to 127 of 127**, and\nthe job that runs it on every pull request is wired.\n\nNothing in `geo-readiness` turned out to be wrong. Every screenshot\ntaken along the way shows the module working — the dashboard rendering,\nthe drawer running its checks, the scores computing. All ~18 faults were\nin the harness and the tests.\n\n```\n✔ authorization.spec.ts        31/31      ✔ report.spec.ts               15/15\n✔ happy-path.spec.ts             8/8      ✔ request-validation.spec.ts   40/40\n✔ rate-limit.spec.ts             2/2      ✔ scope-and-language.spec.ts   31/31\n\n   All specs passed!   127   127   —   —   —     00:50\n```\n\nRuntime went from 8m50s to 50s. Those minutes were timeouts retrying\nagainst assertions that could never match.\n\n## Why none of this was found before\n\n`.github/MIGRATION-NOTES.md` §2.5 deferred the integration-tests job\nbecause `tests/docker-compose.yml` and a provisioning manifest \"did not\nexist\". They had existed for some time. So nothing ever executed this\nsuite, and faults accumulated in it while it looked complete and was\nthoroughly documented — the README even described running `env.run.sh`\nand `set-env.sh`, neither of which was ever committed.\n\nEach fault hid the next. None was findable by reading.\n\n## The harness\n\n- **The image was not published.** `jahia/jahia-dev:8.2.4.0` fails\n`docker manifest inspect` and `docker compose up` with `pull access\ndenied`. Moved to `ghcr.io/jahia/jahia-ee-dev:8-SNAPSHOT`, which the\nsibling harnesses use — and which `tests/README.md` had already named as\nthe alternative.\n- **The whole script layer was missing.** `ci.build.sh`,\n`ci.startup.sh`, `ci.postrun.sh`, `env.run.sh`, `env.debug.sh`,\n`set-env.sh`, `.dockerignore` — added, in the shape `securitytxt` uses.\n`set-env.sh` is deliberately *not* the sibling''s verbatim: its `export\n$(cat .env | sed ''s/=.*//g'' | xargs)` works only because its\n`.env.example` has no comments. Ours documents itself, and measured\nagainst our file that line exported **zero** variables.\n- **`.env.example`** now uses `${NAME:-default}` throughout, so an\nexported value wins and CI can pass its own.\n- **The manifest declared no module dependencies.** There are four:\n`Jahia-Depends: default,jcontent,robots,llms`. `robots` and `llms` do\nnot ship with the platform, so the bundle never resolved and every\nservlet 404''d. `robots` comes from the devtools group; `llms` is not in\nany anonymously reachable Maven repository and is taken from its GitHub\nrelease for now.\n- **The template set could not install.** `dx-base-demo-templates` was\npinned at `2.4.0`, which does not exist at any version in the app store.\nThe release is `4.0.0` in the devtools group, and it needs\n`bootstrap3mix:advancedPagination` from `bootstrap3-core`. The full\nchain is now provisioned, taken from\n`securitytxt/tests/assets/provisioning.yml`.\n- **`cypress-wait-until`** was neither declared nor imported.\n`@jahia/cypress` calls `cy.waitUntil` from\n`PublicationAndWorkflowHelper`, and its own `dist/support/commands.js`\nrequires the plugin — but `registerSupport()` never requires that file,\nso nothing loaded it. Declaring it changed nothing; the import is what\nregisters the command.\n\nWorth noting: **the provisioning API answers HTTP 200 whether or not an\nentry installed.** Three of the faults above were indistinguishable from\nsuccess.\n\n## The fixture\n\n- Granted `editor-in-chief` and `contributor`. **Neither role exists**\non this image, and granting a role that is not there grants nothing, so\nevery user came out with no rights at all. Measured:\n`site-administrator` carries `publish`, `editor` carries\n`publication-start` without it — which is exactly what the\n`CONTRIBUTOR_ROLES` docstring already described. Both overridable now.\n- `assertSitePermission` guarded with `to.not.be.null`, which **passes\nfor `undefined`**, so the next line threw a `TypeError` about JavaScript\ninstead of reporting the permission problem it had correctly caught.\n- The child page was created *before* the site was published and went\nlive with it, while the test asserting \"reports an unpublished page as\nunpublished\" expected the opposite.\n\n## The assertions\n\nThe tests knew the module''s vocabulary — every label matched `en.json`\nexactly — and nothing about its shape:\n\n| asserted | actual |\n|---|---|\n| `lastRun` property | the API returns a `run` object; `lastRun` is\nnowhere in the module |\n| `''home''` in a content-list table | `/pages` opens **Page Builder**, no\ntable; the tree says `''Home''` and `cy.contains` is case-sensitive |\n| click \"Additional\" by label | that accordion is keyed **`apps`** |\n| walk the chrome to the dashboard | it is an admin route:\n`/apps/siteSettingsSeo/geoReadiness` |\n| \"Invisible content\" is a top-level tab | it is a **sub-tab** under\n\"Can a crawler reach it\" |\n| \"Tested URL\" on drawer open | it is a **result**, and lives on\n**Crawler access**, not Score |\n| click a tab to select it | moonstone sets `pointer-events: none` on\nthe active tab, so clicking the tab you are on fails outright |\n\nThree of those cost sixty seconds each before failing.\n\n**One assertion was changed rather than fixed, deliberately.** The\ndrawer''s `/Reachable|Blocked/` is now `/\\d+ of \\d+ checks passed/`. Both\nstrings are in `en.json`, but neither reaches the page once a check has\nrun, so that assertion spent its whole 180s budget against a drawer that\nhad finished and was showing its results. The score line is computed\nfrom the same crawler fetches and proves the same thing — that a result\nrendered rather than a spinner. Flagging it because weakening an\nassertion quietly would be the wrong way to get green.\n\n**The contributor test** now asserts it can reach the SEO section\n*before* asserting the entry is absent from it. `tests/README.md`\nflagged that a red there had two opposite meanings; measured, a\ncontributor is offered Page models and SEO but **not** Link checker, so\nthe section is permission-filtered and the absence is meaningful.\n\n## CI\n\n```yaml\nintegration-tests:\n  needs: build\n  uses: Jahia/jahia-modules-action/.github/workflows/reusable-integration-tests.yml@v2\n  with:\n    module_id: geo-readiness\n    jahia_image: ghcr.io/jahia/jahia-ee-dev:8-SNAPSHOT\n    provisioning_manifest: provisioning-manifest-build.yml\n    instance_type: ubuntu-latest\n```\n\nInputs verified against the live schema of the reusable workflow rather\nthan copied from the note proposing them. `instance_type` matters: the\naction defaults to `self-hosted`, which this repository''s other jobs do\nnot use.\n\n`.github/MIGRATION-NOTES.md` §2.5 and the matching `tests/README.md`\nsection are marked superseded — that stale note is why this never ran,\nand leaving it invites the next reader to re-derive the same wrong\nconclusion.\n\n## Two things to watch\n\n- **A \"Something wrong happened\" banner** appears above the working\ndashboard. It blocks nothing and the dashboard renders correctly beneath\nit, but it is unexplained and is the one loose thread that could be a\nreal defect rather than a test artifact.\n- **CI will be slower than the 50s measured locally**, since it boots\nJahia and provisions 19 modules first. The four jContent UI tests are\nthe ones to watch for flakiness.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <62310815+github-advanced-security[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"a161b9942aecfb5a64d85fd38547b3de55cc2f72","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/a161b9942aecfb5a64d85fd38547b3de55cc2f72"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/efb129acd22ea9d1ab403d66dfe429a0262d60e9","verification":{"payload":"tree a161b9942aecfb5a64d85fd38547b3de55cc2f72\nparent 6e0d7593b066e84db35e9cf5b95143650eb10617\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789488661 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789488661 +0200\n\ntest: make the e2e suite runnable, green, and wired to CI (#12)\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `tests/` Cypress suite went from **0 passing to 127 of 127**, and\nthe job that runs it on every pull request is wired.\n\nNothing in `geo-readiness` turned out to be wrong. Every screenshot\ntaken along the way shows the module working — the dashboard rendering,\nthe drawer running its checks, the scores computing. All ~18 faults were\nin the harness and the tests.\n\n```\n✔ authorization.spec.ts        31/31      ✔ report.spec.ts               15/15\n✔ happy-path.spec.ts             8/8      ✔ request-validation.spec.ts   40/40\n✔ rate-limit.spec.ts             2/2      ✔ scope-and-language.spec.ts   31/31\n\n   All specs passed!   127   127   —   —   —     00:50\n```\n\nRuntime went from 8m50s to 50s. Those minutes were timeouts retrying\nagainst assertions that could never match.\n\n## Why none of this was found before\n\n`.github/MIGRATION-NOTES.md` §2.5 deferred the integration-tests job\nbecause `tests/docker-compose.yml` and a provisioning manifest \"did not\nexist\". They had existed for some time. So nothing ever executed this\nsuite, and faults accumulated in it while it looked complete and was\nthoroughly documented — the README even described running `env.run.sh`\nand `set-env.sh`, neither of which was ever committed.\n\nEach fault hid the next. None was findable by reading.\n\n## 