ts{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/smonier","id":722535,"login":"smonier","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcyMjUzNQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/57f55579fce2382b667345ac51818b0bae0c70d7","node_id":"C_kwDOUVN91NoAKDU3ZjU1NTc5ZmNlMjM4MmI2NjczNDVhYzUxODE4YjBiYWUwYzcwZDc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/geo-readiness/commit/370537921f97810737ddab7ab17f6fe4f27fd4cb","sha":"370537921f97810737ddab7ab17f6fe4f27fd4cb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/370537921f97810737ddab7ab17f6fe4f27fd4cb"}],"sha":"57f55579fce2382b667345ac51818b0bae0c70d7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/57f55579fce2382b667345ac51818b0bae0c70d7"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/722535?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/smonier","id":722535,"login":"smonier","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcyMjUzNQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/smonier","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/commits/370537921f97810737ddab7ab17f6fe4f27fd4cb/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-13T18:24:41Z","email":"smonier@Jahia.com","name":"Stephane Monier"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-13T18:24:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: freshness as a report, plus the test suite and CI the module lacked (#2)\n\n* feat: read freshness as a timeline, one meaning per bar\n\nThe freshness panel drew a range per group, from the newest item to the\noldest with the median marked. It encoded spread, which is not a question\nanybody asks of a site, and on most groups the newest and oldest item share\na date, so the range collapsed to a dot. The lines carried no information.\n\nTwo questions now, one mark each.\n\nA histogram answers when the site was last touched, over a time axis read\nleft to right from oldest to most recent. Height is the only thing encoded.\nA bucket that lies entirely past the chosen threshold carries the warning\ntone, so the line the editor picked is visible in the picture rather than\nonly in a caption.\n\nThe group breakdowns answer how long since anything in the group changed at\nall, sorted worst first. That is the number the threshold is applied to, so\na flagged group and a long bar now say the same thing.\n\nRangeList goes with it, along with its styles: nothing else drew one.\n\n* build: take the patched css-loader and copy-webpack-plugin\n\nBoth pulled transitive packages carrying published advisories: five against\npostcss and two against serialize-javascript. Neither package ships in the\nbundle, so this is the build''s own supply chain rather than the running\nmodule. osv-scanner reports the lockfile clean afterwards, and the front end\nbuilds unchanged.\n\n* build: stop restating manifest headers, and name what the module needs\n\nBundle-SymbolicName and Jahia-Depends were written into the bundle plugin''s\ninstructions while the parent already emits both from properties, so each had\ntwo sources that could disagree. The properties are the source now.\n\nJahia-Depends named only \"default\" while the module hosts its UI in jcontent\nand writes node types owned by robots and llms. Naming them means Jahia holds\nthe module back until they are there, instead of starting it and failing at\nthe first write.\n\n* ci: call the shared Jahia module actions instead of a hand-rolled build\n\nThe workflow was a checkout, a setup-java and one mvn install, so nothing\ngated a pull request beyond compilation: no static analysis, no SonarQube, no\nsignature step, no SBOM and no publication path.\n\nThe workflows now call jahia/jahia-modules-action pinned to its v2 alias, in\nthe shape other Jahia modules use: a pull-request gate, a merge path that\npublishes a snapshot and an SBOM, a release path, and a scheduled full\nanalysis. The org-managed files are copied verbatim and marked as such.\n\nAdds the pull-request template, a dependabot configuration, the Maven\nsettings file every one of those actions expects to find, and the chachalog\nfragment mechanism the repository''s own Changelog property already implies.\n\nMIGRATION-NOTES.md records what this cannot do from inside .github: the\nsecrets and org settings an admin has to provide, the changelog file''s move\nto generated form, the release tag changing shape, and the two lint steps\nleft switched off until the scripts they call exist.\n\n* feat: list every page and when it last changed, paged\n\nThe panel said how much of the site was old and which groups had stopped\nmoving, but never which page. An editor reading it still had to go and find\nthe pages themselves.\n\nThe check now returns one row per published item in the language being\nmeasured: title, type, section, public path, the date it last changed and\nhow long ago that was. Rows are ordered with anything carrying no date first,\nthen oldest to newest, so the list opens on the work.\n\nThe list uses the same paging as every other list here: whole up to\ntwenty-five rows, a control from ten, pages beyond. Each row opens in\njContent. An item past the threshold shows its age in the warning colour,\nbeside the date rather than instead of it.\n\nLong lists are cut at five thousand rows and say so; the counts and the\ngroups above still measure everything.\n\n* test: add the Cypress end-to-end suite the module had none of\n\nTwo releases shipped with no automated test of any kind, so the release gate\nwas a person clicking through jContent.\n\nThe suite covers the three endpoints over three identities: a guest, a\ncontributor holding neither the dashboard''s permission, and a publisher. It\nasserts the premise as well as the result, because a denial proves nothing\nunless the account really does hold the neighbouring rights and only lacks\nthe one being tested. It covers the scope and language values a path-shaped\ninput can take, including a second site keyed so its path is a string prefix\nof the first, request validation per endpoint, the rate-limit window and its\ncarve-out, and the two jContent screens.\n\nSite-per-suite isolation with teardown first, so a run left half-finished\ndoes not poison the next one.\n\nThe suite is not wired into CI yet and has never been run against a live\ninstance; tests/README.md records both, along with the selectors it currently\nnavigates by English label for want of any.\n\n* docs: describe what 1.2.0 changes, and who the dashboard is for\n\nREADME gains a section on who can use each surface, since the endpoints now\nhold to the permission the dashboard route declares and an editor without it\nsees neither the dashboard nor the site-file editors. It also records the\nthree module dependencies, where the fetched address comes from, and the\nend-to-end suite.\n\nThe architecture notes pick up SiteScope, FetchGuard and ScanLifecycle, the\nhistogram replacing the range chart, and the fact that a schedule is owned by\nan account rather than by the trigger alone.\n\nThe check reference describes freshness as it is now drawn, keeps the reason\nthe range chart went, and documents the full per-page list and its cut-off.","tree":{"sha":"786efeedbeda068d2dbbc20fcd98048dae7d8fbb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/trees/786efeedbeda068d2dbbc20fcd98048dae7d8fbb"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/geo-readiness/git/commits/370537921f97810737ddab7ab17f6fe4f27fd4cb","verification":{"payload":"tree 786efeedbeda068d2dbbc20fcd98048dae7d8fbb\nparent d171f7c4b11e920474a3f1514e0d61ec12a18c5b\nauthor Stephane Monier <smonier@Jahia.com> 1789323881 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789323881 +0200\n\nfeat: freshness as a report, plus the test suite and CI the module lacked (#2)\n\n* feat: read freshness as a timeline, one meaning per bar\n\nThe freshness panel drew a range per group, from the newest item to the\noldest with the median marked. It encoded spread, which is not a question\nanybody asks of a site, and on most groups the newest and oldest item share\na date, so the range collapsed to a dot. The lines carried no information.\n\nTwo questions now, one mark each.\n\nA histogram answers when the site was last touched, over a time axis read\nleft to right from oldest to most recent. Height is the only thing encoded.\nA bucket that lies entirely past the chosen threshold carries the warning\ntone, so the line the editor picked is visible in the picture rather than\nonly in a caption.\n\nThe group breakdowns answer how long since anything in the group changed at\nall, sorted worst first. That is the number the threshold is applied to, so\na flagged group and a long bar now say the same thing.\n\nRangeList goes with it, along with its styles: nothing else drew one.\n\n* build: take the patched css-loader and copy-webpack-plugin\n\nBoth pulled transitive packages carrying published advisories: five against\npostcss and two against serialize-javascript. Neither package ships in the\nbundle, so this is the build''s own supply chain rather than the running\nmodule. osv-scanner reports the lockfile clean afterwards, and the front end\nbuilds unchanged.\n\n* build: stop restating manifest headers, and name what the module needs\n\nBundle-SymbolicName and Jahia-Depends were written into the bundle plugin''s\ninstructions while the parent already emits both from properties, so each had\ntwo sources that could disagree. The properties are the source now.\n\nJahia-Depends named only \"default\" while the module hosts its UI in jcontent\nand writes node types owned by robots and llms. Naming them means Jahia holds\nthe module back until they are there, instead of starting it and failing at\nthe first write.\n\n* ci: call the shared Jahia module actions instead of a hand-rolled build\n\nThe workflow was a checkout, a setup-java and one mvn install, so nothing\ngated a pull request beyond compilation: no static analysis, no SonarQube, no\nsignature step, no SBOM and no publication path.\n\nThe workflows now call jahia/jahia-modules-action pinned to its v2 alias, in\nthe shape other Jahia modules use: a pull-request gate, a merge path that\npublishes a snapshot and an SBOM, a release path, and a scheduled full\nanalysis. The org-managed files are copied verbatim and marked as such.\n\nAdds the pull-request template, a dependabot configuration, the Maven\nsettings file every one of those actions expects to find, and the chachalog\nfragment mechanism the repository''s own Changelog property already implies.\n\nMIGRATION-NOTES.md records what this cannot do from inside .github: the\nsecrets and org settings an admin has to provide, the changelog file''s move\nto generated form, the release tag changing shape, and the two lint steps\nleft switched off until the scripts they call exist.\n\n* feat: list every page and when it last changed, paged\n\nThe panel said how much of the site was old and which groups had stopped\nmoving, but never which page. An editor reading it still had to go and find\nthe pages themselves.\n\nThe check now returns one row per published item in the language being\nmeasured: title, type, section, public path, the date it last changed and\nhow long ago that was. Rows are ordered with anything carrying no date first,\nthen oldest to newest, so the list opens on the work.\n\nThe list uses the same paging as every other list here: whole up to\ntwenty-five rows, a control from ten, pages beyond. Each row opens in\njContent. An item past the threshold shows its age in the warning colour,\nbeside the date rather than instead of it.\n\nLong lists are cut at five thousand rows and say so; the counts and the\ngroups above still measure everything.\n\n* test: add the Cypress end-to-end suite the module had none of\n\nTwo releases shipped with no automated test of any kind, so the release gate\nwas a person clicking through jContent.\n\nThe suite covers the three endpoints over three identities: a guest, a\ncontributor holding neither the dashboard''s permission, and a publisher. It\nasserts the premise as well as the result, because a denial proves nothing\nunless the account really does hold the neighbouring rights and only lacks\nthe one being tested. It covers the scope and language values a path-shaped\ninput can take, including a second site keyed so its path is a string prefix\nof the first, request validation per endpoint, the rate-limit window and its\ncarve-out, and the two jContent screens.\n\nSite-per-suite isolation with teardown first, so a run left half-finished\ndoes not poison the next one.\n\nThe suite is not wired into CI yet and has never been run against a live\ninstance; tests/README.md records both, along with the selectors it currently\nnavigates by English label for want of any.\n\n* docs: describe what 1.2.0 changes, and who the dashboard is for\n\nREADME gains a section on who can use each surface, since the endpoints now\nhold to the permission the dashboard route declares and an editor without it\nsees neither the dashboard nor the site-file editors. It also records the\nthree module dependencies, where the fetched address comes from, and the\nend-to-end suite.\n\nThe architecture notes pick up SiteScope, FetchGuard and ScanLifecycle, the\nhistogram replacing the range chart, and the fact that a schedule is owned by\nan account rather than by the trigger alone.\n\nThe check reference describes freshness as it is now drawn, keeps the reason\nthe range chart went, and documents the full per-page list and its cut-off.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqpuppCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAwroQAC36eh94eyGMLrwz+8eXs12L\nJNXmVOBCK4GDBAmgbuNe3BjdB83ftQS1o6t/PVQ7Z46XcXR9kZ4yzGUkuu6tsnOT\nr5I1qA9om+UH8BfhU+u8R3jS/Yk3CbTVts/Py3eo0qd2meDGrLK69+2s+IwLOjI7\n6aha6+QNVUeYmASV/d6zZnE/5DqJwr/P3G84CQYjITog+m4WunP18FkFxGgWS7pG\njcd387/PyIrVE2RljpEyI4TkvjOxDpaCKu/Q014sWLq/+HgE3TWdriw0pjWIC0Tb\ntvgaNVvdmARIkDgVkbu7SJ5dqX3Y/OLZRqPXi86bZQG0oP7bEeZmNFqBV8JIr6Yu\n9CgzTKXQFwlNVk5homRnB31253YKJstT4d1TUM3TbhSW9LDx5w3/uEDFFXy8FEcp\nXVFdr+mr1GfJNRxs+2cdsx5FDEDYq7Uo8qLyeSdA0b2btADI3rM+DK8BfQxIcR1X\ngbYVCgjOVYNgO0bxhFU+e7g9Qk6CX0bz5dQAw+Ubzq77Qr5SiLEt3iDCtw+21s7J\nU7554p6rFSA7fZ2RPBe6vC1at/pLTRjlHExLYohFI8Dnzv+8qzO29gtLFuwHskuj\nP7CF8uqJ0vXpElavRkBxR7xWF7Q87sgPmD7B1igBO13ID7mPMej1vxpMIITomYnw\nFqgutNaCZGeaAX3RUQL+\n=OPVj\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-13T18:24:41Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"http